WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.840
- Bert Spencer:
Give me a minute.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:19.160
If it's fucking important
to me--and this is going

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.320
to start the program.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.120
- Producer: Yes.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.040
- Bert Spencer: Yes?

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.400
- Producer: Yes.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.440
- Bert Spencer:
Alright, you bastards.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:39.400
You're not
beneath--Christ.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.760
Tell me when.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.800
My name is Bert Spencer.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.080
I don't run, and I don't hide,
and I to tend to say it

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.280
as it is, regardless.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.920
Many of you will remember
my name in relation

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:03.400
to the murder
of Carl Bridgewater.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:08.840
I did not kill Carl Bridgewater,
and I am here to prove

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:12.160
to you that I did not.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.520
♪ music playing ♪

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:27.880
- James D'Arcy:
On the 19 of September 1978,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.040
13-year-old Carl Bridgewater
left his home in Woodley

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:32.160
in the West Midlands.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:35.000
Like thousands of other
children across the country,

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.480
he was carrying
out a paper route.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.000
- Carl's Father:
He's a reliable lad.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.560
You know you could always rely
on him to do things, you know,

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.400
never had no worries about
him doing a newspaper route.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.640
- James D'Arcy: At 4 PM, Karl,
was making one of his final

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.360
deliveries of the
day to Yew Tree farm.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.680
The occupants, an
elderly couple, were out.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.720
- "Hello?"

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.560
- James D'Arcy: It seems Carl
entered the farmhouse and

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.520
disturbed a burglary.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.200
Carl was then shot at
point-blank face with a shotgun.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.720
The cold-blooded murder sparked
one of the biggest police

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.080
manhunts for a child
killer ever seen in the UK.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.000
- Bob Stewart: We have
a good witness and a very

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:22.240
clear and lucid
type of witness,

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.600
and he had a
very good close look at a man

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.920
sitting behind the wheel
of what is probably

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.840
a blue estate car.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.800
There is a distinct possibility
that this is one of the men

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.320
that we are looking for.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.120
- James D'Arcy: The police's
suspicions were quickly aroused

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.160
by a local ambulance
officer, Bert Spencer.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:44.800
- Bert Spencer's Daughter: They
told me, that the paperboy,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.600
Carl, has been shot.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.280
And I just sat there, and
I thought, "Oh my God, "

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.320
and my dad was
number one suspect.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.080
- James D'Arcy: But just as
it looked like Bert Spencer

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.840
could've been arrested and
charged with the crime,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.120
events took
an unexpected turn.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.080
Detectives picked up a gang
of robbers for a burglary at

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.480
a nearby farm, known as
the Bridgewater Four,

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.680
they were convicted and
imprisoned for Carl's murder,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.200
but almost immediately,
rumors circulated that

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.120
the men had been
figured out by a corrupt

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.560
police force eager
to solve the crime.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:23.560
A rumor that was proven to
be true when after 20 years,

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.240
the men's conviction
was squashed,

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:26.320
and they were free to go.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.640
With Carl's killer
still at large,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.640
the crime remains one of
the UK's most notorious,

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.080
unsolved murders.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.280
- The one who pulled
the trigger and I wish

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.040
I could
get my hands on him.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:45.520
It's very hard to describe
the feelings to a person.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.760
- James D'Arcy: Although Bert
Spencer was never charged

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.680
with Carl's murder,
he has lived under the constant

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.600
shadow of suspicion.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.280
Now, he has decided
it's time to speak out.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.800
♪ music playing ♪

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.040
- News Reporter: The murder of 13-year-old Carl Bridgewater,

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.640
even by the grizzly
standards of murder itself at

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.760
Yew Tree Farm in Staffordshire,
on the afternoon of September

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.920
1978 was a boy on his after
school newspaper round,

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.640
he unknowingly disturbed an
antique's burglary at this

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:20.080
lonely farm house, and
was brutally shot down.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:22.680
- Carl's Father: It was evening
meal time, like you know,

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:27.520
and we usually sat down to
our evening meal all together,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.280
and Carl hadn't returned,
and Janet got a bit worried,

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:35.920
you see...so I went
to look for him.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.240
When I got down
by Yew Tree Farm,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.920
there was a police
car there, you see,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.880
so I pulled up and asked him
if he had seen a paperboy.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.640
I told him, you know,
and that's when he said there

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.840
had been an incident
with a boy, you know,

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.400
and I had to wait.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.480
♪ music playing ♪

00:04:57.560 --> 00:04:59.360
- James D'Arcy: West Midlands police reconstructed

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.040
Carl's last known
movements in a bid to job

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:04.840
the public's memory
and maybe find an eye-witness

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.120
or other clues.

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.840
- Bob Stewart: I am quite
sure that the offenders went

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.840
to the farm house
to break in and steal

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.960
antiques and as I said,
I'm looking for a link,

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.040
you know,
of similar offenses.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.920
- James D'Arcy: As police began
to gather witness statements,

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.320
there was one in
particular that stood out.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.720
Local company director Roger
Edwards gave a very specific

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.200
account of a vehicle
and its driver.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:32.680
- Roger Edwards: I saw
a pale blue saloon car,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.600
a Vauxhall Viva, turning
into Yew Tree Farm.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.720
I noticed
the driver was a man.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.400
He was wearing a
dark blue uniform.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.840
- James D'Arcy: The police
searched records for all

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:47.080
owners of such a vehicle
in a 50-mile radius.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.880
The only one who wore
a uniform at work was

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:51.800
ambulance officer
Bert Spencer.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:56.880
Once the police realized that
Bert was also a part-time

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:02.440
antiques dealer, he became
a focus of their inquiries.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:08.600
This is an excerpt of the police
interview with Bert in his

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:11.880
kitchen, just days after
the murder of Carl.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.120
- Bert Spencer:
"I'm a married man,

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.920
residing with my wife and
family at the above address.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.440
I'm very interested in
antiques, mainly clocks.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.680
On occasion, I purchase
things for my collection.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:24.600
I'm not a dealer.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:29.680
I'm employed by the West
Midlands Health Authority.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.040
On Tuesday, 19 of September,
I was on duty at the hospital

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.960
from approximately 8:30
until approximately 5:10.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:43.000
I'm the registered owner of a
Vauxhall Viva saloon motorcar."

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.000
- James D'Arcy: In that
first police interview,

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.920
there was some vital
information which Bert chose

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.880
not to tell the police.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.480
Most importantly, that he
had been a neighbor of the

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.880
Bridgewater's for several
years and had regularly hunted

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:00.400
with a shotgun on land
surrounding Yew Tree Farm.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:08.920
- Simon Golding: "Hello,
professor Wilson,

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:10.720
my name is Simon Golding.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:12.720
I'm a journalist and writer,
and have been working on a

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:16.600
book--a biography of sorts,
the man called Bert Spencer.

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:18.800
I was wondering if
you would be interested

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.720
in possibly
interviewing Bert.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.880
Anyway, thank you for
your time and I look forward

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:25.120
to hearing from you.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.840
Bye now."

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:28.880
- David Wilson:
"Hi, Simon.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.240
Thanks for your message.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:35.000
It's professor David
Wilson here, I absolutely

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:39.200
do know of--that Spencer is--

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:40.600
- James D'Arcy:
Criminologist David Wilson

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.120
is used by police forces
across the country

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.760
to get inside the
minds of murderers.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:49.880
- David Wilson: The murder
of Carl Bridgewater is one

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.280
of the most infamous,
unsolved murders in

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.760
British criminal
history and if Bert Spencer

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.440
has agreed
to be interviewed,

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:05.280
that's too good of an
opportunity to miss.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:07.160
- Bert Spencer: Yes,
I did speak to Simon,

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:09.240
and he said
someone might ring.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:11.360
I won't tolerate liars.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.960
I have never been and
there's no need of it.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:15.600
Whatever you have
heard about the case,

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:20.000
you've heard
and nothing yet.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:26.800
- David Wilson: Well, having
read about him for four weeks

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.120
is at least good to
finally see him.

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.280
Quite the extraordinary garden
and he's giving me a wave.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.320
That's very kind of him.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:44.520
Coming out to
meet me, as well.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:45.680
Chatting away.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:50.080
This garden is
quite extraordinary.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.680
Well, I've read
so much about you,

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.400
it's a
pleasure to meet you.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:55.040
I am.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:56.360
It's very
nice to meet you.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:57.160
How are you?

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:58.680
- Bert Spencer: Alright, mate.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:08:59.880
I'm so pleased to meet you.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:00.960
- David Wilson: Well,
it's lovely to meet you.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:01.680
Thank you for asking us.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:02.800
- Bert Spencer: I've heard
a lot of it about you,

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.840
some of it was good.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.920
You're more than welcome
to come in to our home.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.040
- David Wilson: Thank you.

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:10.480
- Christine Spencer:
Hello, I'm Christine.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.200
- David Wilson: Christine,
hello, my name is David Wilson.

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:13.080
It's very nice to meet you.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.840
- Christine Wilson: I've heard
quite a lot about you.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.320
- David Wilson: Oh, well
I hope some of it was

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:17.400
as good as he promised.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:21.680
- Bert Spencer: Some
of it, come on in.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.880
- David Wilson: And what
are all the decorations?

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:26.160
I s this your hobby,
your interest?

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.000
- Bert Spencer: Well,
I love my garden.

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.440
I only have a small garden,
but I do love gardening.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:34.160
Just make it look a
bit tidy, that's all.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:34.840
Come on.

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:35.560
- David Wilson: Thank you.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:38.600
- Bert Spencer: Just
go on in and relax.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.800
You're welcome, go on
in, just go on in there.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.560
- David Wilson: Bert,
are these your paintings?

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:45.680
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.920
Some of them are in here.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:54.160
Some got burnt when my former
wife had a funny and threw

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.320
me out, and she burnt
a lot in the garden.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.160
Lots of things she burnt, but
these are some of them, yes.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:01.160
Yeah.

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.200
- David Wilson: She
burnt your paintings?

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.520
- Bert Spencer:
Burnt a lot of stuff.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:05.840
- David Wilson: Why
would she do that?

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.080
- Bert Spencer: Well, she
went a little bit unwell.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:11.440
And she used to
that now and again,

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.600
and that's one of
those episodes.

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:16.360
Yeah, burnt a lot of stuff.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:17.880
I was at work at the time.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.560
- David Wilson: Also,
you're an ambulance officer.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.080
- Bert Spencer: Oh, that's
from a long time ago, yeah.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.600
I did 19 years with them.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:30.840
The problem is, anything
good you've done in life like

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:33.640
saving lives, delivering
babies, that was lovely.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.640
Helping deliver babies,
that was beautiful.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:37.800
It doesn't count for
anything when you get

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:38.960
yourself in to trouble.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.400
It's all demolished.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:41.760
And it's a shame.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:43.080
really, because it's
still in my mind.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:43.800
It's still in my heart.

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:46.760
I'm still in my heart
an ambulance man,

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:49.280
although physically
I can't be now.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:51.880
- David Wilson: How do
you define yourself then?

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.000
- Bert Spencer:
Just a pensioner.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:54.920
- David Wilson:
Just as a pensioner?

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.320
- Bert Spencer: With no value.

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.360
I'll--I'll qualify
that later on.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:01.200
I have no value no society.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:06.120
Okay, that's what I--that's how
I define myself, but I'm happy.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.040
I'm alive.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.600
I wake up every day and
say, "I'm alive, thank you,

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:14.200
whoever you are."

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.120
- David Wilson:
Well, how about a cup of tea

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.240
and then maybe
start formally having a chat.

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:19.800
- Bert Spencer: You
want a cup of tea?

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:21.760
That's 30 pens.

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:23.280
- David Wilson: I'm
sure I've got it.

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:24.960
I'm sure I've got a 30 p.

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:25.720
- Bert Spencer: Come with me.

00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:26.640
- David Wilson: Thank you.

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:30.240
- Bert Spencer:
Now, throughout this day--

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:31.040
- David Wilson:
The first thing that

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:33.200
I know about
you is that you were born

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:35.680
in December 1939.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:36.640
- Bert Spencer:
In the war years.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.240
- David Wilson: In the war year,
and I just wanted to know

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:42.360
about the family background
that you were born into.

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.360
- Bert Spencer: I
started the war.

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:46.000
I rang it, lad.

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:51.000
I said, "Hey you bastard, you
bombed out our tip shot."

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:52.920
- David Wilson: Can you
describe for me, Bert,

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.720
the relationship that you had
with your mother and father?

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:57.960
Who were you closest to?

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.920
- Bert Spencer: My dad
was a sort of block,

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:04.160
he was allegedly
a hard bastard.

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:05.280
Okay?

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:06.680
I don't hold
a lot against him.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:07.800
- David Wilson:
What did he do?

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:10.280
- Bert Spencer: He used to go
around collecting scrap

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:11.320
iron on the horse and trap.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:17.600
And in those days,
in the market towns,

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:19.000
in the market you'd
have a boxing ring,

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:24.640
and they'd have some sort
of bruiser there, drunk.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.720
And you could go in and if you
could face two round with him,

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.200
you'd get a wide 5 pound note.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:31.840
And my dad used to go in
to get his beer money.

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:34.000
- David Wilson: Your mother
and your father separated?

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.320
- Bert Spencer: They separated.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:37.480
I don't know how old
I was--I really don't.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:39.880
My mother and my sister
and my elders wouldn't

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:43.160
let us talk about
him because we had a granny

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:46.320
sitting in the armchair there, in the Windsor chair.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:49.840
She was in charge of
the house in those days.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:52.440
The granny was in charge, and
you didn't answer granny back,

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:55.520
or else you got a cloud.

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:56.200
Yeah.

00:12:56.240 --> 00:13:00.720
I don't remember a great deal,
except I did love my horse--his

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:05.400
horse and I loved my dad, but
I wasn't allowed to go to him.

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:08.280
He had to come and steal
me when I was playing.

00:13:08.320 --> 00:13:10.200
Mother worked in
a factory, crikey,

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:16.160
God knows how many hours, and we
had a tiny house in Smethwick,

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:19.720
no lights, no fire,
no coal, no money,

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:21.760
and I was put into
Dr. Menardo's home.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.080
- David Wilson:
What was that like?

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:24.840
- Bert Spencer: I was beaten
a bloody savage in there.

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:30.000
We were in a dormitory,
then one day,

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:33.640
I woke up and my
bed was ringing wet.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:37.120
I never, ever wet the
bed and I don't today.

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:42.040
Went down to one
of the breakfast was,

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:44.760
and we all had to line
up, there were nuns there.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.240
Nuns.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:49.040
And we all had to grab a great
big spoon of cod liver oil.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:50.680
"You got to have it.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:52.760
Next."

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:55.480
And I was called
to some office, somewhere,

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:59.800
and I was caned,
bloody Christ.

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:01.040
- David Wilson:
What age were you?

00:14:01.080 --> 00:14:03.640
- Bert Spencer: Maybe 9--10.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:08.160
My hands were on fire, and they
marched me to a big kitchen

00:14:08.240 --> 00:14:11.760
thing, with a great big bell
fast sink and my sheets were

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:14.040
in there with hot water.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:15.720
"Wash those sheets,"

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:17.560
and I kept saying,
"It wasn't me.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.120
I didn't wet the bed."

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:20.880
"How dare you lie?

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:22.720
How dare you lie?"

00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:24.600
- David Wilson: Bert, how
did the sheets get wet?

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:27.600
- Bert Spencer: Well,
I know now--I know now,

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:28.880
there were some
bullies in there.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:29.680
I don't tolerate bullies.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:35.600
One of them peed the
bed, he was in the gang,

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.960
and when we went
out of the room,

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:42.120
he tooled my mattress and
put his mattress and sheets.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.200
And I got--God, the
fire in my hands!

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.720
And while that was
on, I was screaming,

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.560
and I had to go in this
hot water and wash sheets.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.320
- James D'Arcy: With
no formal education,

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:11.040
Bert was forced into a
series of low paid jobs.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:14.680
His life looked like it was
about to drift into petty crime

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:17.680
and a pattern was
beginning to emerge,

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:20.440
but freedom from his harsh
beginnings came following a

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:24.120
chance encounter with an
ambulance driver at a local pub,

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:27.960
which saw Bert sign
up to the service.

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:31.360
For the first time, Bert
felt a sense of belonging,

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:33.920
and relishing the
newfound responsibility,

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:36.440
he would rise rapidly
through the ranks.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:39.640
From ambulance driver to
ambulance liaison officer.

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.240
- David Wilson: What was it
that appealed to you

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:43.120
about ambulance driving?

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:44.480
That's been
a recurring theme.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:46.040
- Bert Spencer: I
know, I don't know.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.400
- David Wilson:
What did you like?

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:48.520
The responsibility?

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:50.360
- Bert Spencer: We used to go
out in the middle of the night,

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:51.320
helping babies.

00:15:51.400 --> 00:15:54.680
We used to stop in the snow
and mom would have a baby.

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:59.080
So, I took a course to make sure
I'd never do anything wrong.

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:02.160
The baby would survive my
rough handling as it were.

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:05.680
Time and time again, in
the middle of the night,

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:08.840
in the snow and the baby would
come along and I'd--I did

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:12.640
whatever I could, and eventually
we'd get to a hospital,

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.440
and these little
things, soaking wet,

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:20.840
covered in blood and this
tiny thing, I got life.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:26.200
And I don't know what it is.

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:28.120
I'm getting emotional.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:36.560
- David Wilson: I know you are.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.360
You-- - Bert Spencer:
Can we just wait a bit?

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:39.960
- David Wilson: Yes, yes.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:43.200
Of course!

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:48.440
You tell me when you
feel ready to start.

00:16:58.560 --> 00:16:59.840
- Bert Spencer: Come on.

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:09.480
- David Wilson: It's fine, we're
going through emotional stuff.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:14.960
- I have come here
to stop this lie.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.160
I had no involvement in
the Carl Bridgewater murder,

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:20.320
I did not kill Carl.

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.200
No involvement whatsoever.

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:26.840
- James D'Arcy: Since 1978,
the murder of Carl Bridgewater

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:30.440
has cast a long shadow
across Bert's life.

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:33.360
His determination to clear
his name once and for all,

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:36.360
has been the driving force
behind allowing David Wilson

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:37.640
to interview him now.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:42.160
- David Wilson: Let's talk about
the death of Carl Bridgewater.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:43.560
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:44.560
- David Wilson: Where were
you when you first heard

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:46.720
the news that Carl Bridgewater
had been murdered?

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:48.640
- Bert Spencer: At home.

00:17:48.720 --> 00:17:49.880
No, sorry, sorry!

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:51.480
I was at
work the next day.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:56.440
- David Wilson:
How did you react?

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:57.880
- Bert Spencer: Bloody hell!

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:00.760
- David Wilson: When you
were first interviewed--

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:01.720
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:04.040
- David Wilson: By the police,
here was this shocking

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.840
murder of a paperboy.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.360
You knew the farm.

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:11.520
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:12.960
- David Wilson: You
knew the family.

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:13.680
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:15.160
- David Wilson:
The Bridgewater's,

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:17.800
but you didn't really offer
very much to the police

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:19.360
in that first interview.

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:20.760
- Bert Spencer: I knew them.

00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:21.400
They weren't
personal friends.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.040
I know you.

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:24.120
You're not
a personal friend.

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:28.440
So, if you got stabbed
tomorrow in Boston,

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.360
I don't have much to offer.

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:33.200
I knew you, yes.

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:35.680
- David Wilson: There is
a witness who says that

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:37.960
he saw a Vauxhall Viva.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:38.680
- Bert Spencer: Right.

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:39.880
- David Wilson: Which
you had at the time.

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:40.960
- Bert Spencer: Right.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:43.400
- David Wilson: And the person
driving the Vauxhall Viva

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:46.120
was wearing a uniform
with two pips on it.

00:18:46.160 --> 00:18:48.080
- Bert Spencer: Thank
you for saying that.

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:52.360
If you went down to Yew
Tree Farm, down the lane,

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:54.960
and saw a Vauxhall
Viva turning left,

00:18:55.000 --> 00:18:57.600
you'd see the back of a
Vauxhall Viva turning left,

00:18:57.640 --> 00:18:59.800
you'd think nothing
else whatsoever.

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:04.640
You just mentioned a car that
could have been mine, it wasn't.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:08.640
- James D'Arcy: In order
to check Bert's statement,

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:13.320
police visited his workplace,
Colbert Hospital in Woodsley.

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:15.520
They were particularly
interested in the alibi,

00:19:15.560 --> 00:19:18.160
which his secretary,
Barbara, provided.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:22.640
- Barbara: "I can confirm
that Mr. Hubert Spencer was

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:24.800
at Colbert Hospital
all afternoon."

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:28.080
- James D'Arcy:
What Bert didn't tell the police

00:19:28.160 --> 00:19:30.440
was that he and
Barbara were close friends

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:32.480
and neighbors.

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:35.320
- Bert Spencer: Someone said,
it didn't tell the police that

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:40.040
Barbara lived ten
doors up the same road.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:41.680
- David Wilson: She was
the secretary that you worked

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:42.680
with at the hospital.

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:44.000
- Bert Spencer: She was a
secretary at the hospital.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:45.480
They didn't ask.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:51.520
You know, I could have said,
"I've got green socks tonight.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:53.440
I just had a poo in the
toilet," they didn't ask.

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:55.400
And I appreciate what
they were there for,

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:59.360
they were asking questions
and I answered the questions.

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:00.760
I answered every question.

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:05.400
- David Wilson: You continue to
campaign against people thinking

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:07.520
that you had anything
to do with that crime.

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.560
- Bert Spencer: I've
stopped that now.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:11.640
Listen, I'm on camera.

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:13.720
I don't give a shit.

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:14.520
Think what you will.

00:20:14.600 --> 00:20:16.600
♪ music playing ♪

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:21.920
- James D'Arcy:
Despite the growing body

00:20:21.960 --> 00:20:23.920
of circumstantial evidence
mounting against Bert,

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:26.680
a major breakthrough came in
the case when the police picked

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:29.040
up a gang of burglars who
would rob a nearby farm.

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:31.400
- Reporter: Police
picked up four men.

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:33.560
All known to them
as armed robbers.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:37.880
Michael Hickey, Vincent, his
cousin and James Robinson.

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:39.880
A fourth, Pat Molloy.

00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:42.040
- James D'Arcy: After
several days of interrogation,

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:44.240
one of the men,
Patrick Molloy,

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:46.960
admitted to being
at Yew Tree Farm.

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:49.600
He said he was upstairs
when he heard a gunshot

00:20:49.680 --> 00:20:52.080
and when his accomplice,
16-year-old Michael

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:55.360
Hickey had accidentally
killed Carl with a shotgun.

00:20:55.440 --> 00:20:58.720
Molloy's apparent confession led
to the rest of the gang turning

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:00.000
on each other.

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:03.040
This led to the successful
prosecution of the so-called

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:06.360
Bridgewater Four who
were found guilty and jailed

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:09.960
for between 12-25
years for the crime,

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.200
but there was one
person who was convinced

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:14.560
that they were innocent.

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:17.480
- Then as the teenager,
Michael Hickey, was led away,

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:19.840
his mother Anne, rushed to
the front of the gallery

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:23.560
and cried down tearfully,
"I'll fight, Michael.

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:24.680
I'll fight."

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:27.320
- James D'Arcy:
Michael Hickey's mother,

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:30.800
Anne Wheelan, began a
20-year-long campaign to free

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:34.120
the men and discover who
Carl's real killer could be.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:40.000
And just four weeks after the
Bridgewater Four were jailed,

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:42.240
there was an
extraordinary development,

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:48.920
which immediately raised new
questions about who killed Carl.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:52.800
- Reporter: Here, at
Holloway House Farm, a .5

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:56.080
mile from Yew Tree, again,
another brutal shotgun killing.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:57.760
This time the victim was
a 70-year-old farmer

00:21:57.840 --> 00:21:58.480
called Hubert Wilkes.

00:21:58.560 --> 00:21:59.880
In the middle of a late
night social gathering,

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:01.920
Wilkes' close friend,
Hubert Spencer left the room,

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.800
returned with a
shotgun and killed him.

00:22:04.880 --> 00:22:11.280
♪ music playing ♪

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:12.160
- James D'Arcy:
The newsboy and the farmer

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:14.480
were killed in
neighboring farm houses,

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:17.200
and the coincidences
didn't end there.

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:21.440
Just like Carl, Hubert Wilkes,
was killed with a shotgun.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:24.000
Just like Carl, he was shot
in the head at close range.

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:28.880
Just like Carl, he was
murdered while seated.

00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:32.560
One man knew both
properties and both victims,

00:22:32.640 --> 00:22:36.720
and that man was Bert Spencer.

00:22:36.760 --> 00:22:39.280
♪ music playing ♪

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:42.240
Bert has no
explanation for why he murdered

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:45.000
Wilkes, but he does have
some memories of the night he

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:49.120
killed his best friend
and father figure.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:50.920
- Bert Spencer: Little old man,
I loved him because he

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:52.200
was like a father.

00:22:52.280 --> 00:23:01.040
♪ music playing ♪

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:03.320
- James D'Arcy:
On December 13, 1979,

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:05.880
Hubert Wilkes hosted some
drinks at Holloway House Farm

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:09.160
for Bert's 40 birthday.

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:11.360
Present were farmer
Wilkes, his daughter Jean,

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:14.280
Bert and his wife, Janette.

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:16.640
- Bert Spencer: He got the
feeling that I was better

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:20.120
than his son, and eventually,
he started brining me

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:22.200
into the family.

00:23:23.560 --> 00:23:25.920
A neatly and devout,
and cooked me a

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:28.280
Sunday lunch.

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:32.160
So, he was like a father figure
and that's where it hurts.

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:35.560
Now, my first wife, used
to do his bookkeeping.

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:42.080
She had said to me, she will
deny this, and I don't care.

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:44.120
She had said to me,
"Don't leave me

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.160
along with him."

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:53.320
Previously, had said to me, "Janette is being so loud.

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:55.960
I put my hand on her
thigh, and she wants me,

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:57.240
and doesn't
fucking want you."

00:23:57.320 --> 00:24:01.120
He gave me all this,
all for about two years,

00:24:01.200 --> 00:24:02.800
I took it.

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:03.440
I took it.

00:24:03.520 --> 00:24:09.080
Don't talk, it's just an old
man, but he was like a father.

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:14.480
I hadn't gotten my own dad and
then, we went down that night,

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:16.680
he came out all
bloody and blinding,

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:19.320
and the dog got to be
grabbed and put away.

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:22.680
Then he went and took down
the thing to another room

00:24:22.760 --> 00:24:23.720
to do the books.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:27.760
I sat in the lounge with
Jane, just general chat.

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:31.200
And Jane said, "It
is your birthday,

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:33.200
I bought you a present.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:36.200
A little bit of pipe
tobacco, duty free."

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:36.880
"Thank you, Jane, "

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:39.240
I didn't kiss her,
I didn't love her.

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:41.120
I didn't want her.

00:24:41.200 --> 00:24:44.680
She was sisterly-like.

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:48.960
Just a lovely girl, she was
studious, extremely proud.

00:24:49.040 --> 00:24:52.480
When she was dressed
up, very proud.

00:24:52.520 --> 00:24:53.560
"Thank you, love,
that's nice."

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:58.440
Pipe tobacco,
I smoked a pipe and previously,

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:01.480
Hubert Wilkes used
to brag--the farmers

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:06.320
used to get together,
and he'd mix special cocktails

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:09.240
to get their women pissed
and then have a swap around.

00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:17.280
Thought nothing of it, but I
must've thought something of it.

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:19.200
I'd had a lot of whiskey
before I went in there

00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:20.720
because it was my birthday.

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:22.920
I'd done a lot of whiskey.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:24.720
I used to drink whiskey,
I don't know,

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:25.920
in case it makes me nasty.

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:31.320
Poured a drink, then he
stood up, and then he says,

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:35.760
in a great, big, loud voice,
I was nearly pissed now.

00:25:35.800 --> 00:25:39.360
"Janette, I'd made
you a special cocktail."

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:44.840
And I cannot remember
what happened next, except,

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:47.680
I think I
fell down in a hallway,

00:25:50.680 --> 00:25:52.240
and it was flash,
bang, wallop.

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:54.160
I know I feel down,
I do know.

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:58.440
Went out, apparently,
while I was out there,

00:25:58.520 --> 00:26:02.200
I took his gun from the
door jamb, went to my car,

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:04.280
got a hack saw,
cut the barrel off.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:12.960
I went in and Janette and Jean
said, "He walked in the room,

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:15.960
and rolled around the room.

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:18.040
Upright."

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:21.720
Then I went and shot him.

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:25.480
- James D'Arcy:
Burt's wife, Janette,

00:26:25.560 --> 00:26:28.760
returned home in shock
and covered in blood.

00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:31.120
She was met by her
terrified teenage daughter.

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:35.040
Bert's daughter is able to
recall what her mother said

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:37.640
the night her father
murdered Hubert Wilkes.

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:40.640
She has asked
not to be filmed.

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:44.200
"My mother said she got
up from the sitting,

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:46.400
and they had a fight.

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:47.920
As she was trying to
grab the gun off him,

00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:52.760
he used the butts of
the gun and just constantly

00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:55.520
hit her on
the top of her head.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:27:00.600
And she ran off, and she ran
into the kitchen and my dad was

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:08.680
chasing her through her house,
and she hid under the table."

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:10.640
- Bert Spencer: I picked another
gun up, from another door jamb,

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:13.360
put it in my car,
I drove up the lane

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:14.160
and saw an ambulance.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:19.400
"Oh, my mage,"
and I stopped.

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:20.320
I stopped.

00:27:20.400 --> 00:27:23.240
It's all insane.

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:25.240
And a driver came up,
and he said,

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:31.240
"Bert, don't go down the lane, there's a gunman on the loose."

00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:35.520
I said, Barry,
"I think it's me."

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:40.640
- James D'Arcy:
The death of Hubert Wilkes

00:27:40.720 --> 00:27:42.920
saw Bert in
the dark for his murder.

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:45.960
- Bert Spencer: Okay, I
deserved to be put away,

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:48.000
but it wasn't intent.

00:27:48.080 --> 00:27:49.000
I did what I did.

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:52.520
I'm so bloody sorry I did it,
but I didn't go there with a

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:55.120
gun, thinking,
"I'll kill the bastard."

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:56.560
It's just
something that bang,

00:27:56.640 --> 00:28:00.120
it happened
because I was pissed.

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:03.920
Throughout his trial, Bert
Spencer, remained silent.

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:06.920
He never took the stand
and no explanation was ever

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:09.920
given for why he
had killed the farmer.

00:28:09.960 --> 00:28:12.680
But watching the proceedings
closely was a woman with

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:14.280
a vested
interest in the case.

00:28:14.320 --> 00:28:17.520
- Bert's Daughter: I remember
a lady looking at me,

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:20.640
every day, and smiling.

00:28:20.720 --> 00:28:22.960
And it was Anne Wheelan.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:28.000
She latched on to the
possibility that my father

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:30.400
could've been responsible
and not her son,

00:28:30.440 --> 00:28:32.560
for killing the child.

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:36.080
- James D'Arcy: Bert was
sentenced to life in prison.

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:38.880
The striking similarities
between the murder of Carl

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:42.640
and Hubert Wilkes soon
became public knowledge.

00:28:42.720 --> 00:28:45.920
This fueled the determination
of the Bridgewater Four campaign

00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:48.000
group and their lawyers.

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:51.160
- There are a number
of coincidences,

00:28:51.240 --> 00:28:53.520
which if there hadn't
been that second murder,

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:56.960
at the farm up the road, one
may never had known about it.

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:58.800
- James D'Arcy: One
of the condemned men,

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:02.640
started the longest rooftop
protest in prison history.

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:05.960
- Michael Hickey: Demonstrated
on the roof of Lestechers's

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:08.720
top security quarry prison.

00:29:08.800 --> 00:29:10.080
Stating his case through a makeshift loud halo.

00:29:10.160 --> 00:29:12.600
- James D'Arcy: Shouting his
innocence from the rooftops,

00:29:12.680 --> 00:29:15.160
Hickey also yelled
the name of the man he

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:20.240
believed was guilty of
Carl's murder, Bert Spencer.

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:25.680
By now, Wheelan had
convinced the investigative

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:28.880
journalist Paul
Foote to join her cause.

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:32.240
With his help, Bert's name was
never out of the headlines.

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:36.200
Often, openly being accused
as Carl's real killer.

00:29:36.240 --> 00:29:38.600
- Bert Spencer: These
alleged, what did I call them?

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:40.400
Celebrities.

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:43.480
The campaign group, it's
full of celebrities,

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:47.400
all trying to get me
hung for the crime.

00:29:47.480 --> 00:29:49.080
They didn't know what
they were talking about,

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:51.880
but they--some of those
groups came to wear Wormwood

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:54.520
scrubs at 11:30
one night and outside,

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:57.360
and outside the big wall,
with a razor on it,

00:29:57.440 --> 00:29:59.960
chanting up on the--you
can say, the windows.

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:02.520
Shouting, "Kill Bert
Spencer, Kill Bert Spencer.

00:30:02.600 --> 00:30:03.400
Go Bridgewater,"

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:04.920
inciting the
inmates to kill me.

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:10.000
And you, members of that
campaign group, judge me.

00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:19.640
I won't name you here, I
won't name and shame you here.

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:22.320
I may do, one day.

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:24.360
I may.

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:30.880
- James D'Arcy: Bert claims
that the barrage of accusations

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:37.200
meant his own life
was in constant danger

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:40.600
whilst in prison,
and he was left with no option,

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:42.840
but to turn
to violence or die

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:44.520
at the hands
of other inmates.

00:30:44.600 --> 00:30:47.280
- David Wilson: You were
a convicted murderer,

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:51.800
you--body builder, were you
the top of the hierarchy?

00:30:51.880 --> 00:30:55.240
Were you the--the
daddy of the way?

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:57.200
- Bert Spencer: Not purposely.

00:30:57.280 --> 00:30:58.640
Not purposely.

00:30:58.720 --> 00:30:59.920
No, I used to protect
people, there are bullies.

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:02.200
There's actually rapists in
male prisons, did you know that?

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:03.320
- David Wilson: Yeah.

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.920
- Bert Spencer:
You must know that.

00:31:05.960 --> 00:31:07.440
There--barbaric bastards.

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:16.200
♪ music playing ♪

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:18.400
We had a young Philippine
kid that was raped

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:21.520
in our adult wither ,
3-4 got a hold of him

00:31:21.600 --> 00:31:27.280
and--I went to town.

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:29.360
- David Wilson: What did you do?

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:31.240
- Bert Spencer: I just went
in like a bloody bulldozer.

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:33.240
I dealt with it.

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:34.760
I stopped it.

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:38.080
When somebody goes
off a young kid,

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:42.840
and they got long hair and
bloody pigtails and shaved

00:31:42.920 --> 00:31:45.440
in ras clad, the bumba
clad, I went in, mate.

00:31:45.520 --> 00:31:47.520
I just went--I don't know.

00:31:47.600 --> 00:31:48.640
I didn't ever think about it.

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:52.160
I just went in and--I'm not
proud of what I had to

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:54.680
do in my sentence.

00:31:54.760 --> 00:31:59.160
On occasions, I did it.

00:31:59.240 --> 00:32:01.840
I did it, and I stopped it.

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:05.200
And then I got a reputation,
I ran the bloody prison.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.840
I just wasnt'
afraid of anyone.

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:12.160
- James D'Arcy: Whilst Bert says
he ruled the other inmates

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:16.200
with an iron fist, something
were beyond his control.

00:32:16.280 --> 00:32:22.000
- Bert Spencer: When I was in
Wormwood scrubs, my dad died.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:23.680
And they took me out,
all the way to Birmingham,

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:26.440
Sully Oak, to the funeral.

00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:28.120
Handcuffed all the way,
you know about this

00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:30.200
because you
were the governor.

00:32:31.680 --> 00:32:35.800
When I got there, the three
screws that were escorting me.

00:32:35.880 --> 00:32:39.480
Total strangers, they didn't
know and they said,

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:41.720
"Right, Bert.

00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:43.760
We don't know you.

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:46.880
Don't do anything
stupid."

00:32:49.200 --> 00:32:50.520
I said,
"What are you talking about?

00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:51.840
I'm not going
to do anything stupid."

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:53.200
They said, "Well,
we're going to uncuff you."

00:32:53.240 --> 00:32:54.600
I said, "Do it."

00:32:55.880 --> 00:33:03.320
♪ music playing ♪

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:04.680
Throughout the service,
the priest kept staring

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:07.160
at me.

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:09.960
I'm not paranoid,
and I didn't mind that,

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:11.920
and the priest came to me
afterwards, and he said,

00:33:12.000 --> 00:33:16.600
"When I went and saw the coffin,
I thought I was looking at you."

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:20.880
- James D'Arcy:
Whilst in prison,

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:23.800
Bert claims that he was
continually silenced

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:26.520
and unable to defend himself against accusations

00:33:26.600 --> 00:33:28.360
that he murdered Carl.

00:33:28.400 --> 00:33:33.080
This feeling of being ganged at
the time, continues to rile him.

00:33:33.160 --> 00:33:36.440
- Bert Spencer: I've
waited 30 years for this.

00:33:36.480 --> 00:33:39.480
I've been suppressed, I've
been gagged, I've been shot on,

00:33:39.560 --> 00:33:40.600
kicked while I was down.

00:33:40.680 --> 00:33:43.480
I'm standing up now.

00:33:43.520 --> 00:33:46.000
Come and have a kick!

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:46.680
I'm different now.

00:33:46.720 --> 00:33:51.520
- David Wilson: Will it be
light for you if people who

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:55.880
watch the program listen
to what you're arguing.

00:33:55.960 --> 00:33:59.280
Weigh it all up
and still say,

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:02.000
"Yeah, he killed
Carl Bridgewater."

00:34:02.040 --> 00:34:02.840
- Bert Spencer: Okay.

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:07.280
Can you come forward with the
slightest fragment of evidence?

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:09.560
Because we deal with
evidence in courts.

00:34:09.640 --> 00:34:12.400
We don't deal
with newspapers.

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:13.480
We deal with evidence.

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:16.280
Can you prove it?

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:20.360
But I am standing up now,
you can't trample over me.

00:34:20.440 --> 00:34:24.120
Be careful--what
you say against me.

00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:26.720
I might
retaliate in the courts.

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:30.640
- David Wilson: I want
to come back and reflect.

00:34:30.720 --> 00:34:33.880
After I've reflected on this
first meeting and talked

00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:36.640
to you about other
things that struck me

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:38.280
about the conversation
we've had today.

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:39.440
Is that okay?

00:34:39.520 --> 00:34:40.280
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:41.320
- David Wilson: Great.

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:44.040
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:47.520
- David Wilson: Well,
thank you very much, Bert,

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:49.520
for agreeing to see me,
and I'll see you again soon.

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:50.920
- Bert Spencer: You're
more than welcome anytime.

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:52.960
- David Wilson: Thank you
and let's reflect on some

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:54.560
of the things
that we discussed.

00:34:54.640 --> 00:34:56.920
Bits that--you know, we
need to think about again.

00:34:56.960 --> 00:35:00.640
All the bits we need to cover,
and I'll look forward

00:35:00.720 --> 00:35:01.760
to seeing you.

00:35:01.840 --> 00:35:04.080
- Bert Spencer: Look forward to
seeing you again, safe journey.

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:05.800
- David Wilson: Thank you.

00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:07.480
- Bert Spencer: Come up, here.

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:08.760
- David Wilson: Take care.

00:35:08.840 --> 00:35:09.800
Bye, Christine.

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:11.400
- Bert Spencer: Safe journey.

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:12.200
- Christine: Bye.

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:14.520
Bye.

00:35:14.600 --> 00:35:16.640
- David Wilson: Go in!

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:23.160
It's cold.

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:25.560
I'm developing a
picture of Bert.

00:35:25.640 --> 00:35:28.120
I don't want to
easily jump to labels,

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:32.200
but there were some
huge narcissism.

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:34.760
Some huge--"I'm going
to speak to the nation.

00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:42.920
I'm going to have my
justification for my behavior."

00:35:43.440 --> 00:35:48.000
We need to check the materials
in relation to his movements

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:51.000
on the day that Carl
Bridgewater died.

00:35:51.080 --> 00:35:55.440
I, therefore, would quite
like to speak to Barbara.

00:35:55.520 --> 00:36:02.280
He was going to mention her as
quickly as possible and move on,

00:36:02.360 --> 00:36:08.440
and yet, this was a woman
who basically has stood

00:36:08.520 --> 00:36:12.720
up his alibi about
being in Colbertl Hospital

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:17.560
on the day Carl Bridgewater died, for decades!

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:22.880
So, it's her, really, I'd
be quite keen to talk to.

00:36:22.960 --> 00:36:28.480
Given that Bert is so
extroverted about other

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:30.920
people in the story.

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:34.520
Barbara, no matter how central,
mentioned and then ignored,

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:36.880
very quickly.

00:36:36.960 --> 00:36:38.360
I quite like to speak to her.

00:36:38.440 --> 00:36:43.320
Bert--Bert is just coming
behind us with another dog.

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:46.320
So, do you know whose
dog it is, Bert?

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:50.640
- Bert Spencer: We're going
to find out, op there.

00:36:50.680 --> 00:36:51.600
Come on, babe.

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:52.240
In there, love.

00:36:52.320 --> 00:36:53.160
In there.

00:36:53.240 --> 00:36:57.480
- David Wilson: That's what is
going at his mind at the minute.

00:36:57.520 --> 00:36:59.480
He wants to know
exactly what I think.

00:36:59.560 --> 00:37:02.200
He wants to know if
he passed the test.

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:04.000
There he goes.

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:08.040
The hero in his own mind.

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:11.200
Animal lover, child lover.

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:12.200
Killer.

00:37:12.280 --> 00:37:16.960
♪ music playing ♪

00:37:17.000 --> 00:37:20.000
- James D'Arcy:
As a convicted murderer,

00:37:20.080 --> 00:37:23.360
Bert served the full
term of 14 years for killing

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:24.640
Hubert Wilkes.

00:37:24.720 --> 00:37:28.240
He received no parole because
he would never satisfactorily

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:32.080
explain why he had
murdered the farmer.

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:35.840
On his release, Bert,
drifted away into obscurity,

00:37:35.920 --> 00:37:39.120
but any hopes he had of a
quiet life were dashed when

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:41.760
the Bridgewater Four's
conviction was quashed,

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:43.560
and they were
made free men.

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:46.600
[INDISTINCT SPEECH]

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:56.080
And it was the circumstantial
evidence against Bert Spencer,

00:37:56.160 --> 00:37:59.520
which featured prominently in
the defense counsel's arguments

00:37:59.600 --> 00:38:01.040
during the court appeals.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:02.680
- Female Reporter: Mr.
Bennet Heightner, QC,

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:05.280
spoke about a man seen by
police after the murder,

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:07.080
called Herbert Spencer.

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:09.880
Mr. Spencer had also failed
to disclose that he knew

00:38:09.920 --> 00:38:12.200
Carl Bridgewater,
that he bought and sold

00:38:12.280 --> 00:38:15.000
antiques and that he
had permission to shoot

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:17.120
birds at Yew Tree Farm.

00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:20.760
- James D'Arcy: With Carl's
killer still walking free,

00:38:20.840 --> 00:38:23.880
Bert Spencer, once again,
found himself being asked

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:26.840
difficult questions about
the newspaper boy's death.

00:38:26.920 --> 00:38:29.680
- Male Reporter: Joining me now
are Bert Spencer who has been

00:38:29.760 --> 00:38:33.040
accused of killing Carl
Bridgewater, his wife Christina,

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:35.600
and Anne Wheelan,
Michael Hickey's mother.

00:38:35.680 --> 00:38:38.280
Do you now feel the need
to get involved in the search

00:38:38.320 --> 00:38:39.080
to find that man.

00:38:39.160 --> 00:38:40.280
- Anne Wheelan I won't rest.

00:38:40.360 --> 00:38:43.560
Let's put it like this,
until the perpetrator of that

00:38:43.640 --> 00:38:46.440
child--the killing of
that child is behind bars.

00:38:46.520 --> 00:38:50.000
I mean, he has been
accused of this by prisoners,

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:51.960
seven times, at least.

00:38:52.040 --> 00:38:54.280
- Male Reporter: Now, after
reading that, this morning,

00:38:54.360 --> 00:38:56.360
a lot of people are going
to draw the conclusion

00:38:56.400 --> 00:38:58.760
that you killed
Carl Bridgewater.

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:00.120
- Bert Spencer: A
lot of people are,

00:39:00.200 --> 00:39:01.960
but nothing keeps the spotlight
off me while there is

00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:04.880
people like Anne Wheelan
pointing her finger at me.

00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:07.760
I do not fear any
police investigation,

00:39:07.840 --> 00:39:08.960
I would welcome one.

00:39:09.040 --> 00:39:11.240
- Anne Wheelan: It wasn't
me that accused you in

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:12.120
the very first place.

00:39:12.200 --> 00:39:13.240
I want to finish, please.

00:39:13.280 --> 00:39:15.320
Is this not a fact?

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:16.360
You did have guns.

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:18.960
Is this not a fact, you
were an antique dealer?

00:39:19.040 --> 00:39:21.920
Is this not a fact, you
lived next door to the child,

00:39:21.960 --> 00:39:23.840
and you refused to
tell the police that?

00:39:23.920 --> 00:39:27.040
Is this not a fact that
your car was seen turning

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:28.280
into Yew Tree Farm?

00:39:28.360 --> 00:39:31.680
The police went out and in
a 50-mile radius to look

00:39:31.760 --> 00:39:35.760
for Vauxhall Viva, they came
out with a few, Vauxhall Viva.

00:39:35.840 --> 00:39:38.800
Is this a fact that
you were the only man in

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:40.640
uniform that was
found and it happened to

00:39:40.720 --> 00:39:42.840
be the man--is this
a fact when they went

00:39:42.880 --> 00:39:44.320
to your house,
and you could've said,

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:45.920
"Go and see my colleague
Mrs. --,

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:47.680
she will give you an alibi."

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:50.600
Is this not a fact,
your papers have gone missing?

00:39:50.680 --> 00:39:51.920
- Bert Spencer: Don't
lie on this program.

00:39:52.000 --> 00:39:53.880
- Anne Wheelan: She
went to the hospital.

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:57.120
Mr. Spencer, I have this,
and I don't need papers.

00:39:57.160 --> 00:40:01.960
I have--well, I'm sorry,
this has to be sorted out.

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:03.280
- Male Reporter:
This is not a court.

00:40:03.360 --> 00:40:04.640
- Bert Spencer: Alright.

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:05.320
- Male Reporter: Neither
is it trial by media,

00:40:05.400 --> 00:40:07.400
but let me
ask you one question.

00:40:07.480 --> 00:40:08.920
For the record--

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:10.480
- Bert Spencer: Alright, ask it.

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:12.680
- Male Reporter: Did you
shoot Carl Bridgewater?

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:16.280
- Bert Spencer: I did not
and that is on the record

00:40:16.360 --> 00:40:18.760
and no attempts by these people,
nor a mountain of attempts

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:21.400
is going to remove
it from the record.

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:24.160
Talking about facts,
that, lady, is fact.

00:40:24.240 --> 00:40:26.480
- James D'Arcy: During the
course of the campaign to

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:29.120
free the Bridgewater
Four, Anne Wheelan,

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:32.640
accumulated thousands
of legal documents.

00:40:32.720 --> 00:40:35.480
She believed they may
hold clues to help catch

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:37.760
Carl Bridgewater's killer.

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:40.960
David Wilson has been
given unrestricted access

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:42.240
to the materials.

00:40:42.320 --> 00:40:45.720
- David Wilson: And are all
these boxes related to the case?

00:40:45.800 --> 00:40:47.640
- Anne Wheelan: Yes, cabinets.

00:40:47.720 --> 00:40:51.120
All those boxes, and the boxes
all behind and everything.

00:40:51.200 --> 00:40:54.960
It's crystal clear in some
of these boxes that you see,

00:40:55.040 --> 00:40:58.200
that the evidence is there
to convict the person that's

00:40:58.280 --> 00:41:02.560
responsible for
the horrific murder.

00:41:02.600 --> 00:41:05.960
Yeah.

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:09.520
Psychopath, I think he is.

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:15.040
So, that's what
I'm hoping, anyway.

00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:20.200
- David Wilson: Anne, you've
been incredibly vocal about

00:41:20.240 --> 00:41:25.400
saying that Bert Spencer was
responsible for Carl's murder.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:27.520
Why do you think
he's never sued you?

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:30.360
- Anne Wheelan:
Because he did it!

00:41:30.440 --> 00:41:31.360
Because he's guilty.

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:36.080
If he sues me, he takes me into
a court of law, doesn't he?

00:41:36.160 --> 00:41:40.960
- David Wilson: The quashing of
those convictions, inevitably,

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:43.600
put the spotlight back
on to Bert Spencer.

00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:47.200
You met him a few times,
how did those encounters go?

00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:51.240
- Anne Wheelan: I find
him, personally, again,

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:57.000
a personal feeling this
is, quite a chilling man.

00:41:57.080 --> 00:42:00.880
- David Wilson: How does
Bert Spencer react to you

00:42:00.960 --> 00:42:02.520
on a personal level?

00:42:02.600 --> 00:42:04.800
- Anne Wheelan: Oh, most of
the time, wishes me dead.

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:06.960
It's the best thing
that could happen to me.

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:10.920
So, and I'll tell
you why, I think,

00:42:10.960 --> 00:42:13.000
because he knows
that I'm right.

00:42:13.080 --> 00:42:18.440
I've got a feeling he
wants to be challenged.

00:42:18.520 --> 00:42:21.400
He knows he's guilty,
I think he's playing

00:42:21.440 --> 00:42:23.080
a game with everybody.

00:42:23.160 --> 00:42:24.640
With everyone.

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:28.480
- David Wilson: Why is he
brining it all back up now,

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:30.240
after all these years?

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:33.800
- Anne Wheelan: I think
he wants to be found out.

00:42:33.880 --> 00:42:35.640
He wants to be found out.

00:42:35.680 --> 00:42:38.320
- David Wilson: Do you think
he's trying to confess?

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:39.680
- Anne Wheelan: He
wants to confess,

00:42:39.720 --> 00:42:42.600
but he wants somebody to
really--like he's--like

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:45.960
he's playing a tough
game with people,

00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:48.920
he wants that tough game
played back with him.

00:42:48.960 --> 00:42:52.480
He wants it dragged out
of him because he's not

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:54.560
normal in the head.

00:42:54.600 --> 00:42:56.080
He's not a normal person.

00:42:56.160 --> 00:43:00.960
I know, I just know,
I'm not just pointing a

00:43:01.040 --> 00:43:05.560
finger because of the sake
of it, but in my eyes,

00:43:05.640 --> 00:43:08.480
Bert Spencer killed
Carl Bridgewater.

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:12.440
- Bert Spencer: I don't want
to speak to Anne Wheelan.

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:15.640
I have no interest,
whatsoever, in my life.

00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:18.160
She is not even part of
my life, she's a nothing.

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:23.520
I actually hate the bitch.

00:43:23.600 --> 00:43:26.440
I worship the ground
she has coming to her.

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:28.800
Horrible bitch.

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:31.680
Her boy did it.

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:33.240
I am absolutely convinced.

00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:36.400
♪ music playing ♪

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:38.280
- James D'Arcy:
Coming up next time.

00:43:38.360 --> 00:43:40.560
Professor Wilson learns more
about Bert's association with

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:43.240
the place
where Carl was killed.

00:43:43.680 --> 00:43:44.600
- David Wilson: The
police officer says,

00:43:44.680 --> 00:43:46.080
"Have you visited
Yew Tree Farm?"

00:43:46.120 --> 00:43:48.640
Spencer replied, "Yes, I have.

00:43:48.720 --> 00:43:51.560
You found out."

00:43:51.640 --> 00:43:54.080
- James D'Arcy:
David and Bert embark on a

00:43:54.160 --> 00:43:57.120
remarkable road trip, returning
to the scene of the crime.

00:43:57.200 --> 00:43:59.040
- David Wilson: What do
you think Carl might say

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:00.040
back to you?

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:01.040
- Bert Spencer: I don't know.

00:44:01.120 --> 00:44:02.000
I really don't.

00:44:02.080 --> 00:44:02.720
What do you think he might say?

00:44:02.800 --> 00:44:04.400
- David Wilson: He might
say you killed him.

00:44:04.440 --> 00:44:06.080
- James D'Arcy: And the relation
between the two men

00:44:06.160 --> 00:44:07.640
reach breaking point.

00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:10.080
- Bert Spencer: Is he trying to
tie me into Carl Bridgewater,

00:44:10.120 --> 00:44:11.920
and it feels
as though he is.

00:44:12.000 --> 00:44:13.120
I wasn't' t there.

00:44:13.200 --> 00:44:15.920
I wasn't guilty, and now
he's raising it all up again.

00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:18.760
That criminalizes me.

00:44:18.840 --> 00:44:20.320
I could be bloody arrested.

00:44:20.640 --> 00:44:24.440
criminologist professor
David Wilson riles Bert as he

00:44:24.520 --> 00:44:27.240
questions him about whether
he had any role in the murder

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:28.560
of Carl Bridgewater.

00:44:28.640 --> 00:44:30.040
- Bert Spencer:
Listen, I'm on camera.

00:44:30.080 --> 00:44:32.080
I don't give a shit.

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:33.440
Think what you will.

00:44:33.480 --> 00:44:36.760
- James D'Arcy: Professor Wilson
becomes convinced that

00:44:36.840 --> 00:44:39.120
Bert's alibi
demands closer scrutiny.

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:44.160
- David Wilson: This was a woman
who basically has stood up

00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:47.680
his alibi about being
in Colbert Hospital,

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:52.400
on the day that Carl
Bridgewater died for decades.

00:44:52.440 --> 00:44:54.680
- James D'Arcy: And could
Bert be playing a game

00:44:54.760 --> 00:44:57.040
of psychological chess
with the professor?

00:44:57.120 --> 00:44:58.760
- Anne Wheelan: He
knows he's guilty.

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:01.960
I think he's playing
a game with everybody.

00:45:08.920 --> 00:45:14.080
- Bert Spencer: That should go
in the compost with many people,

00:45:14.120 --> 00:45:16.920
you just strip the leaves
off, and you plant it,

00:45:16.960 --> 00:45:18.840
and you will produce a life.

00:45:18.920 --> 00:45:20.120
A living thing.

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:21.120
I don't want to lose it.

00:45:21.200 --> 00:45:24.720
I've cut it now, I'll just
poke it in there, look.

00:45:24.800 --> 00:45:26.640
I appreciate life,
living things,

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:30.040
human beings
and life itself.

00:45:30.080 --> 00:45:30.680
Okay?

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:32.040
I took a life.

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:33.000
Okay?

00:45:33.040 --> 00:45:36.440
I'm fully aware of
the awfulness of that.

00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:38.000
♪ music playing ♪

00:45:38.080 --> 00:45:43.160
Okay?

00:45:43.240 --> 00:45:46.000
- James D'Arcy: On
December 13, 1979,

00:45:46.080 --> 00:45:48.640
just four weeks after the
Bridgewater Four had been

00:45:48.720 --> 00:45:52.480
jailed, another murder took
place at a Farmhouse just

00:45:52.560 --> 00:45:56.040
500 yards from where
Carl was murdered.

00:45:56.120 --> 00:46:00.360
The victim was local farmer
Hubert Wilkes, but this time,

00:46:00.400 --> 00:46:03.080
the killer was caught at
the scene of the crime.

00:46:03.160 --> 00:46:08.680
His name is Bert Spencer.

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:11.480
The murder of Hubert Wilkes
was almost a carbon copy

00:46:11.520 --> 00:46:12.520
of the murder of Carl.

00:46:12.600 --> 00:46:15.680
Both victims were shot in the
side of the head while seated,

00:46:15.720 --> 00:46:18.040
at point-blank range
with the shotgun,

00:46:18.120 --> 00:46:22.480
but Bert believes he was made
a scapegoat for Carl's murder

00:46:22.560 --> 00:46:24.520
by the campaign group
determined to see

00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:27.360
the Bridgewater Four
released, and he has strong

00:46:27.440 --> 00:46:29.720
views on
forgiving and forgetting.

00:46:29.800 --> 00:46:33.800
- Bert Spencer: We go to
church--I go to church.

00:46:33.880 --> 00:46:36.360
That's hard to believe
for some of you.

00:46:36.440 --> 00:46:41.400
I go to church and I say, with
you, the Lord's prayer, okay.

00:46:44.480 --> 00:46:46.520
Oh, the wind blew that up.

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:48.320
Look.

00:46:48.400 --> 00:46:54.640
Now, I have to question the rest
of the world, why do you say it?

00:46:54.720 --> 00:47:00.080
Why do you--why are you two
faced liars when you talk

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:03.120
to your God concept?

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:05.200
Why on earth in the
Lord's prayer, do you say?

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:08.560
"Dear Lord, our
Father in Heaven.

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:10.120
Hallowed be thy name--"

00:47:10.200 --> 00:47:13.440
and then further
down the prayer,

00:47:13.520 --> 00:47:18.520
"Forgive us our trespasses,"
good, "And forgive us,

00:47:20.520 --> 00:47:23.440
as we forgive those
who trespass against us."

00:47:25.520 --> 00:47:28.720
What a load of
liars we are, as a nation.

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:33.360
We will not forgive those
who trespass against us,

00:47:33.400 --> 00:47:35.760
we want revenge.

00:47:35.800 --> 00:47:37.000
No one gets forgiven.

00:47:37.040 --> 00:47:38.800
Why do we have courts?

00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:40.520
Prosecutions?

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:43.640
We don't forgive, but
we say to our God,

00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:47.360
"We will forgive those who
trespass against us."

00:47:47.440 --> 00:47:52.000
All of those people, who
joined a campaign group,

00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:57.080
to persecute me and mentally
torture me for all those years,

00:47:57.120 --> 00:47:58.360
why didn't you forgive me?

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:00.440
I've never done
anything against you.

00:48:02.360 --> 00:48:03.800
I think that answers
the question.

00:48:03.880 --> 00:48:10.560
You're liars, you are two
faced, as you talk to your God,

00:48:10.600 --> 00:48:13.200
and he will do something
about it one day.

00:48:13.240 --> 00:48:16.640
♪ music playing ♪

00:48:16.720 --> 00:48:19.960
- James D'Arcy: During
the course of the campaign

00:48:20.040 --> 00:48:22.920
to free the
Bridgewater Four, Anne Wheelan,

00:48:23.000 --> 00:48:25.320
the mother of one of
those wrongly accused,

00:48:25.400 --> 00:48:28.680
accumulated thousands
of legal documents.

00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:32.200
She believes they may help
catch Carl Bridgewater's killer

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:34.920
and has handed them over
to criminologist professor

00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:36.240
David Wilson.

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:39.360
- David Wilson: Now, are all
these boxes related to the case?

00:48:39.440 --> 00:48:40.360
- Anne Wheelan: Yes.

00:48:40.440 --> 00:48:41.800
- James D'Arcy: Professor Wilson
has been invited to speak to

00:48:41.880 --> 00:48:44.880
Bert, who is determined to
put to rest rumors that he was

00:48:44.960 --> 00:48:47.600
involved in Carl's murder.

00:48:47.640 --> 00:48:51.360
With an archive of over 10,000
documents in his possession,

00:48:51.440 --> 00:48:54.520
David Wilson has assembled a
team of criminology researchers

00:48:54.600 --> 00:48:57.920
to begin the critical and
painstaking task of sifting

00:48:58.000 --> 00:49:00.400
through the documents in
order to identify anything of

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:03.560
importance that could lead
him closer to understanding

00:49:03.640 --> 00:49:06.520
what happened to Carl,
the day he was killed.

00:49:06.600 --> 00:49:10.120
- David Wilson: This is
old-fashioned research.

00:49:10.160 --> 00:49:14.000
In these boxes are documents
collected over the course of

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:18.000
20 years by Anne Wheelan,
who is the mother of

00:49:18.080 --> 00:49:21.480
Michael Hickey,
one of the Bridgewater Four.

00:49:21.560 --> 00:49:24.680
The documents are
trial transcripts,

00:49:24.760 --> 00:49:28.760
police witness statements,
newspaper cuttings,

00:49:28.840 --> 00:49:30.680
prison correspondence.

00:49:30.760 --> 00:49:34.840
Frankly, they are a treasure
trove of information

00:49:34.920 --> 00:49:39.240
and I believe that in
these boxes is the identity

00:49:39.320 --> 00:49:42.760
of the killer
of Carl Bridgewater.

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:45.400
- James D'Arcy:
Searching the papers,

00:49:45.480 --> 00:49:48.600
the researcher soon uncover
documents that professor

00:49:48.680 --> 00:49:51.680
Wilson believes may
be of real importance.

00:49:51.760 --> 00:49:54.880
- David Wilson: This is
the very first statement

00:49:54.960 --> 00:49:57.040
that Spencer is
going to make to the police

00:49:57.120 --> 00:50:00.280
in relation to the murder
of Carl Bridgewater

00:50:00.360 --> 00:50:01.520
and this is the alibi.

00:50:01.600 --> 00:50:02.800
Here is his alibi.

00:50:02.880 --> 00:50:06.760
8:30 until 5:10.

00:50:06.840 --> 00:50:10.520
He's claiming he was at
the hospital all day.

00:50:10.560 --> 00:50:11.720
That's his alibi.

00:50:11.760 --> 00:50:17.760
This alibi is what
protects Bert Spencer.

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:22.800
- James D'Arcy: Bert Spencer has
always relied on one piece of

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:25.120
evidence, when there is any
suggestion that he may have

00:50:25.200 --> 00:50:27.720
been involved in the killing
of Carl Bridgewater.

00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:30.560
The fact that his
secretary, Barbara,

00:50:30.640 --> 00:50:32.960
provided him with
a cast iron alibi.

00:50:33.040 --> 00:50:37.000
Bert worked as an ambulance
officer at Colbert Hospital

00:50:37.080 --> 00:50:40.240
and his secretary Barbara
gave a statement saying that

00:50:40.280 --> 00:50:44.040
he had been
at work all day.

00:50:44.120 --> 00:50:46.720
- Melinda: David, I
just got something here.

00:50:46.760 --> 00:50:50.960
Earlier on, Barbara said
that Bert wouldn't

00:50:51.040 --> 00:50:52.720
ever leave the hospital,

00:50:52.760 --> 00:50:55.280
but it's mentioned here that
he might leave for lunch

00:50:55.360 --> 00:50:59.600
around 1 PM or
either earlier or later--

00:50:59.640 --> 00:51:03.280
- David Wilson: Melinda,
this is very important.

00:51:03.360 --> 00:51:08.960
Look, except for, perhaps,
leaving for lunch!

00:51:09.040 --> 00:51:12.280
"Sometimes he had lunch a little
bit earlier, or a bit later,

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:14.520
and he may have
done on that day.

00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:17.800
He was upset and
worried at the time,

00:51:17.880 --> 00:51:21.680
by the attention given to him
by the police."

00:51:21.760 --> 00:51:23.960
This is really important, Melinda, well done.

00:51:24.040 --> 00:51:25.560
♪ music playing ♪

00:51:25.640 --> 00:51:28.160
- James D'Arcy: The ongoing search of the documents

00:51:28.200 --> 00:51:31.400
turns up another
compelling piece of information.

00:51:31.440 --> 00:51:34.320
All ambulance officers who
worked at Colbert Hospital kept

00:51:34.400 --> 00:51:38.240
timesheets in a log filed
away at the end of each month.

00:51:38.320 --> 00:51:41.200
They provided the exact
whereabouts of employees in

00:51:41.240 --> 00:51:43.440
the jobs they
were succumbed to.

00:51:43.480 --> 00:51:46.600
These logs could've
supported Bert's alibi,

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:49.000
had they not
mysteriously disappeared.

00:51:49.080 --> 00:51:51.880
- A police officer has gone
and looked at the ambulance's

00:51:51.920 --> 00:51:54.600
daily record sheets
and unfortunately

00:51:54.680 --> 00:51:57.520
on the date
of Carl's murder,

00:51:57.560 --> 00:52:00.520
the record
sheets are missing.

00:52:00.560 --> 00:52:04.000
- James D'Arcy: Bert as a
senior ambulance officer,

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:06.600
was one of the few people at
Colbert Hospital who would've

00:52:06.680 --> 00:52:09.400
had access to these records.

00:52:09.440 --> 00:52:12.400
He maintains that corrupt police
officers could have removed

00:52:12.440 --> 00:52:14.840
the records and logbooks
in a bid to make him a

00:52:14.880 --> 00:52:17.120
scapegoat for Carl
Bridgewater's murder.

00:52:20.360 --> 00:52:23.040
As the search through
the documents continues,

00:52:23.120 --> 00:52:26.200
unearthed is the complete
police transcript of the second

00:52:26.280 --> 00:52:30.720
interview conducted with
Bert at Colbert Hospital.

00:52:30.760 --> 00:52:33.480
Taking place almost a week
after the first interview,

00:52:33.560 --> 00:52:37.080
Bert's opening line to
detectives suggests that he was

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:39.080
expecting to see them again.

00:52:39.160 --> 00:52:41.960
- David Wilson: Go back
to the very start of it.

00:52:42.040 --> 00:52:42.640
- The very start.

00:52:42.680 --> 00:52:45.360
- David Wilson: Look what
he says here that the police

00:52:45.440 --> 00:52:48.880
officer says, "I made known
to him the reason for my visit

00:52:48.920 --> 00:52:51.680
and said, "Have you
visited Yew Tree Farm?

00:52:51.720 --> 00:52:54.480
Spencer replied, "Yes, I have.

00:52:54.560 --> 00:52:55.560
You found out."

00:52:55.640 --> 00:52:58.720
- James D'Arcy:
When asked if he'd ever been

00:52:58.800 --> 00:53:02.080
to Yew Tree Farm in
uniform, Bert replied,

00:53:02.160 --> 00:53:03.600
"I think so."

00:53:03.680 --> 00:53:05.760
In his first
statement, Bert Spencer,

00:53:05.840 --> 00:53:08.560
didn't tell police that he had
at one point lived only a

00:53:08.600 --> 00:53:11.440
few doors away from
Carl Bridgewater.

00:53:11.520 --> 00:53:14.760
In his second interview,
the police pushed Bert on how

00:53:14.800 --> 00:53:16.600
well they knew each other.

00:53:16.640 --> 00:53:19.960
- David Wilson: That's a
very odd statement, isn't it?

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:20.800
- Yeah.

00:53:20.840 --> 00:53:23.000
- David Wilson: "I
didn't recognize him, "

00:53:23.080 --> 00:53:26.480
is he meaning the press
photographs or is he actually

00:53:26.560 --> 00:53:30.480
in Yew Tree Farm that day?

00:53:33.000 --> 00:53:37.640
Does Carl Bridgewater have to
die for the simple instrumental

00:53:37.720 --> 00:53:40.840
reason that Bert
didn't recognize him,

00:53:40.920 --> 00:53:44.000
but Carl recognized Bert.

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:47.160
- James D'Arcy:
Professor Wilson wonders,

00:53:47.200 --> 00:53:50.240
could this have been the true
motive for Carl's murder?

00:53:55.680 --> 00:54:00.680
- David Wilson: Well, I wondered
if we were going to find

00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:11.560
these in the boxes,
at some stage.

00:54:11.640 --> 00:54:18.040
These are actually the crime
scene photographs themselves

00:54:18.120 --> 00:54:21.520
of Carl after
he had been murdered.

00:54:21.600 --> 00:54:25.040
So, they are
truly appalling.

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:28.480
So, I think
we'll just stop.

00:54:28.560 --> 00:54:30.480
She shouldn't
be filming this.

00:54:30.520 --> 00:54:40.200
♪ music playing ♪

00:54:40.280 --> 00:54:42.800
- James D'Arcy:
Having been through Anne

00:54:42.880 --> 00:54:44.760
Wheelan's files,
professor Wilson,

00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:47.560
returns to speak
to Bert once more.

00:54:47.600 --> 00:54:49.960
Determined to ask him
about the new information

00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:51.080
he has uncovered.

00:54:51.160 --> 00:54:53.440
- David Wilson: I see you
got your wainscot again,

00:54:53.520 --> 00:54:54.720
that's good.

00:54:54.800 --> 00:54:56.400
- Bert Spencer: Are you
jealous you haven't gotten one?

00:54:56.440 --> 00:55:00.520
- David Wilson: Very jealous.

00:55:00.600 --> 00:55:02.360
- Bert Spencer: That's
a different wainscot,

00:55:02.440 --> 00:55:03.680
I got about a dozen,
I love them

00:55:03.720 --> 00:55:07.680
- David Wilson: Do you think your wainscot and your garden

00:55:07.760 --> 00:55:10.720
tell the same
story about the man

00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:14.000
who is Bert Spencer?

00:55:14.080 --> 00:55:17.400
You like to stand out
from the crowd, don't you?

00:55:17.480 --> 00:55:19.160
- Bert Spencer: No,
no, no, no, no, no, no.

00:55:19.240 --> 00:55:22.000
I'm just--I'm old-fashioned,
that' a wainscot is.

00:55:22.080 --> 00:55:25.400
I'm made edwardian
maybe Victorian, no

00:55:25.440 --> 00:55:28.760
edwardian , and the garden
is just--that's my hobby.

00:55:28.840 --> 00:55:30.840
I don't stand out.

00:55:30.880 --> 00:55:34.040
- David Wilson: You don't
think this stands out from

00:55:34.120 --> 00:55:35.520
the other gardens
in the village?

00:55:35.600 --> 00:55:37.680
- Bert Spencer: Yes, it does.

00:55:37.720 --> 00:55:41.200
That wasn't the idea
when I built it.

00:55:41.280 --> 00:55:44.880
This is a cycling route, and
you get crowds of cyclists,

00:55:44.920 --> 00:55:49.000
and we get
people from Denmark.

00:55:49.080 --> 00:55:53.040
They stopped there, about three
days ago, and one of them said,

00:55:53.120 --> 00:55:54.760
"He has a whole museum!"

00:55:54.800 --> 00:55:57.440
- David Wilson: It's true.

00:55:57.520 --> 00:55:58.640
- Bert Spencer: But it
wasn't designed for that.

00:55:58.720 --> 00:56:00.960
It's just, I like
it, it's lovely.

00:56:01.000 --> 00:56:02.720
Christine likes it.

00:56:02.800 --> 00:56:06.320
I like it,
I like gardening.

00:56:06.400 --> 00:56:07.360
- David Wilson: Where
can we go for a walk.

00:56:07.440 --> 00:56:08.120
- Bert Spencer: At the park!

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:11.360
- David Wilson: Come on
then, you lead the way.

00:56:11.440 --> 00:56:12.840
I kept coming back
to two things.

00:56:12.880 --> 00:56:16.120
Why now?

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:18.640
Why do you want to
talk about this now?

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:20.800
- Bert Spencer: I wanted
to talk about it 30 years

00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:23.840
ago but was suppressed.

00:56:23.920 --> 00:56:24.520
I couldn't!

00:56:24.560 --> 00:56:25.640
I wasn't' allowed to.

00:56:25.720 --> 00:56:26.520
- David Wilson: Yeah?

00:56:26.560 --> 00:56:28.000
Who wouldn't allow
you to talk about it?

00:56:28.080 --> 00:56:29.080
- Bert Spencer: The prison.

00:56:29.160 --> 00:56:31.880
In ---, I had a bundle
of papers that were

00:56:31.960 --> 00:56:33.600
going to be
what was in my manuscript.

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:38.120
Things I had replied, noted,
and they snatched them

00:56:38.200 --> 00:56:38.800
out of my hand.

00:56:38.840 --> 00:56:41.760
You are not allowed to
write or say about prison,

00:56:41.840 --> 00:56:44.120
or anybody else's crime
or your own crime.

00:56:44.200 --> 00:56:46.880
Does that
answer your question?

00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:48.520
I wasn't allowed to.

00:56:48.600 --> 00:56:49.200
I am today.

00:56:49.280 --> 00:56:50.120
I'm free.

00:56:50.160 --> 00:56:52.120
- David Wilson: Why do you
think they wanted to cover up?

00:56:52.160 --> 00:56:55.400
- Bert Spencer: I think they
were really sure--was Bert

00:56:55.480 --> 00:56:57.720
involved, wasn't he?

00:56:57.760 --> 00:56:58.560
That's the feeling I got.

00:56:58.600 --> 00:56:59.960
I thought it was me
against the world.

00:57:00.040 --> 00:57:00.680
I really did.

00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:02.440
Even the coppers.

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:04.760
I thought it was me against
the world and that's how

00:57:04.800 --> 00:57:06.080
I lived for a few years.

00:57:06.160 --> 00:57:08.720
I was at
war against the world.

00:57:08.800 --> 00:57:10.360
- David Wilson: Do you
think you're a bit paranoid?

00:57:10.440 --> 00:57:11.040
- Bert: No.

00:57:11.120 --> 00:57:12.200
I think I'm not.

00:57:12.240 --> 00:57:13.400
- David Wilson: Being at
war, "Me against the world, "

00:57:13.440 --> 00:57:14.440
is paranoia.

00:57:14.520 --> 00:57:16.040
- Bert Spencer: Yeah, but
look at every newspaper.

00:57:16.080 --> 00:57:18.880
Look--when I did get a chance
to see a television, I'm on it.

00:57:18.960 --> 00:57:23.120
It's people that like saying,
"The records are missing."

00:57:23.200 --> 00:57:23.960
- Bert Spencer: So, what?

00:57:24.040 --> 00:57:25.040
I'm in prison.

00:57:25.120 --> 00:57:25.920
Someone took them.

00:57:26.000 --> 00:57:27.920
- David Wilson: The
records--these are the records

00:57:28.000 --> 00:57:30.960
that would have shown your
movements on the day that

00:57:31.040 --> 00:57:34.880
Carl Bridgewater
was murdered and those records

00:57:34.960 --> 00:57:39.200
by going missing weren't
able to corroborate what

00:57:39.240 --> 00:57:41.200
it was that you
said about where you were

00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:43.880
because your argument
is that you were at Colbert

00:57:43.960 --> 00:57:44.680
Hospital all day.

00:57:44.760 --> 00:57:46.680
- Bert Spencer: But
they weren't necessary.

00:57:46.760 --> 00:57:48.560
A lot was made of them.

00:57:48.640 --> 00:57:50.600
- David Wilson: What
was in the records?

00:57:50.680 --> 00:57:52.920
What might have been
something that could

00:57:53.000 --> 00:57:54.000
exonerate you completely?

00:57:54.080 --> 00:57:55.200
- Bert Spencer: Yes, yes.

00:57:55.280 --> 00:57:58.440
- David Wilson: Something in
the book--might have been

00:57:58.520 --> 00:57:59.800
something that would have
incriminated you completely.

00:57:59.880 --> 00:58:00.480
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

00:58:00.520 --> 00:58:02.480
- David Wilson: So, the records
going missing is quite important

00:58:02.560 --> 00:58:03.800
in terms of the story that
we've been discussing.

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:05.280
- Bert Spencer: It is,
but if there was something

00:58:05.360 --> 00:58:08.360
in that book that
could incriminate me,

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:11.080
why didn't they give
it to the news media?

00:58:11.160 --> 00:58:12.280
"We got him!"

00:58:12.360 --> 00:58:14.320
- David Wilson: Because
it was you that took it.

00:58:14.400 --> 00:58:15.760
That's their argument!

00:58:15.840 --> 00:58:17.840
Their argument is that it
was you that took the book.

00:58:17.880 --> 00:58:19.160
- Bert Spencer: You
couldn't get in.

00:58:19.240 --> 00:58:19.840
You can't get in.

00:58:19.880 --> 00:58:21.920
- David Wilson: But you knew
the headquarters really well,

00:58:22.000 --> 00:58:23.280
didn't you?

00:58:23.320 --> 00:58:24.400
- Bert Spencer: I
knew it really well.

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:25.280
- David Wilson: You definitely
were at Colbert Hospital

00:58:25.320 --> 00:58:26.280
all day.

00:58:26.360 --> 00:58:27.080
- Bert: All day.

00:58:27.160 --> 00:58:27.960
- David Wilson: All day?

00:58:28.040 --> 00:58:31.120
- Bert: Every minute of
that day and it was proven, so--

00:58:31.160 --> 00:58:34.000
- David Wilson: Well, it
hasn't been proven because

00:58:34.080 --> 00:58:35.960
the thing that could
prove that Bert,

00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:39.760
were the records and the
records went missing.

00:58:39.800 --> 00:58:43.360
- James D'Arcy: Bert had
a cast iron alibi at the time

00:58:43.400 --> 00:58:45.520
in his secretary Barbara.

00:58:45.600 --> 00:58:48.720
She was able to testify that
Bert had been at Colbert

00:58:48.800 --> 00:58:52.400
Hospital all day on the
day Carl was killed.

00:58:52.440 --> 00:58:54.640
This would see the heat
of the police investigation

00:58:54.680 --> 00:58:56.320
taken off Bert.

00:58:56.400 --> 00:58:59.360
- David Wilson: Bert, were you
always faithful to your wives?

00:58:59.440 --> 00:59:00.800
- Bert Spencer: No.

00:59:00.880 --> 00:59:02.520
- David Wilson:
You played around?

00:59:02.600 --> 00:59:03.880
- Bert Spencer: I played around
with someone I thought

00:59:03.920 --> 00:59:05.360
I had fallen in love with.

00:59:05.440 --> 00:59:07.440
- David Wilson: Is
that woman still alive?

00:59:07.520 --> 00:59:08.520
- Bert Spencer: I don't know.

00:59:08.560 --> 00:59:10.040
I don't know.

00:59:10.120 --> 00:59:13.960
I mean, I've made no attempt
to rekindle as it were,

00:59:14.000 --> 00:59:14.880
I don't know.

00:59:14.960 --> 00:59:16.880
- David Wilson: What
period of time was that?

00:59:16.960 --> 00:59:18.880
When were
you having an affair?

00:59:18.960 --> 00:59:22.800
- Bert Spencer: Just before
I committed my offense.

00:59:22.840 --> 00:59:25.560
- David Wilson: So,
in the late 70s?

00:59:25.640 --> 00:59:27.040
- Bert Spencer: I
was getting on a bit.

00:59:27.120 --> 00:59:29.120
- David Wilson: So that was
when you were still married

00:59:29.200 --> 00:59:30.440
to your
first wife, Janette?

00:59:30.480 --> 00:59:32.360
- Bert: Yes.

00:59:32.440 --> 00:59:33.040
Yes.

00:59:33.080 --> 00:59:34.480
- David Wilson: Bert, will
you be even more honest with

00:59:34.520 --> 00:59:36.320
me and tell me who you were
having an affair with?

00:59:36.400 --> 00:59:38.520
- Bert Spencer: No,
because--no, I won't.

00:59:38.600 --> 00:59:40.600
- David Wilson: Is
this woman still alive?

00:59:40.680 --> 00:59:41.560
- Bert Spencer: I don't know.

00:59:41.640 --> 00:59:42.880
I really don't know, no.

00:59:42.960 --> 00:59:44.360
- David Wilson: Was
it a work colleague?

00:59:44.400 --> 00:59:45.000
- Bert Spencer: No.

00:59:45.080 --> 00:59:47.400
She was lovely.

00:59:47.480 --> 00:59:52.480
We melted, and we parted,
and she was so lovely,

00:59:54.920 --> 00:59:56.680
I wouldn't upset
her life, you know?

00:59:56.760 --> 00:59:58.160
It was lovely.

00:59:58.240 --> 00:59:59.240
It was lovely, it really was.

00:59:59.320 --> 01:00:01.200
- David Wilson: I'm worried
that you're getting cold.

01:00:01.240 --> 01:00:02.880
- Bert Spencer: I am cold, yes.

01:00:02.960 --> 01:00:04.280
- David Wilson: So, let's
go back to the house.

01:00:04.320 --> 01:00:05.080
- Bert Spencer: I am cold.

01:00:05.160 --> 01:00:07.280
Yeah.

01:00:07.320 --> 01:00:08.720
- James D'Arcy:
Back in the house,

01:00:08.760 --> 01:00:10.640
David Wilson gets the
chance to speak to Bert's

01:00:10.720 --> 01:00:13.320
current partner, Christine.

01:00:13.400 --> 01:00:17.280
- Christine: He said, I've got
something I've got to tell you.

01:00:17.360 --> 01:00:23.400
He said, I've been
in prison for murder.

01:00:23.480 --> 01:00:28.480
First off, I didn't know
really which way to think

01:00:30.640 --> 01:00:35.880
because I never came across
somebody like this before,

01:00:35.960 --> 01:00:40.960
and being the first time,
I was a bit apprehensive.

01:00:44.320 --> 01:00:50.840
I didn't know if Bert was
still the same person or

01:00:50.920 --> 01:00:55.320
if his lifestyle had changed.

01:00:55.400 --> 01:01:04.240
Whether he was still violent
or if he was still in the same

01:01:06.120 --> 01:01:08.240
mood, sort of thing.

01:01:08.280 --> 01:01:15.280
When Bert explained things to
me, what he--what he had done,

01:01:17.720 --> 01:01:22.320
and then when the
police contacted him,

01:01:22.360 --> 01:01:28.800
it was--they were putting 2
and 2 together and making 5,

01:01:29.960 --> 01:01:36.760
but they couldn't deduct
anything from it because things

01:01:36.840 --> 01:01:44.520
were going, dropping into place,
which I'd say as a --not as a

01:01:45.840 --> 01:01:52.040
policeman or a detective, but
I would have said, "Blue car,

01:01:52.120 --> 01:01:54.160
Bert's got a blue car.

01:01:54.240 --> 01:01:58.360
Person in uniform,
Bert wears a uniform.

01:01:58.440 --> 01:02:00.600
Knows the family?

01:02:00.680 --> 01:02:03.200
Bert knows the family."

01:02:03.280 --> 01:02:06.600
He knew where
they lived, well,

01:02:06.680 --> 01:02:14.000
3/3 makes a story.

01:02:14.040 --> 01:02:16.080
- David Wilson: Who
is Bert Spencer?

01:02:16.160 --> 01:02:19.520
- Bert Spencer: Bert Spencer
is, I am my daddy's boy.

01:02:19.600 --> 01:02:23.280
He was a nasty,
hard bastard.

01:02:23.320 --> 01:02:25.240
- David Wilson: Are
you a nasty, hard?

01:02:25.320 --> 01:02:25.960
- Bert Spencer: That's me.

01:02:26.040 --> 01:02:27.080
- David Wilson: Are you
a nasty, hard bastard?

01:02:27.120 --> 01:02:30.680
- Bert Spencer: No, but
I'm his boy, aren't I?

01:02:30.760 --> 01:02:32.520
- David Wilson: So, the apple
never falls too far

01:02:32.560 --> 01:02:33.600
from the tree?

01:02:33.680 --> 01:02:34.680
Is that
what you're arguing?

01:02:34.760 --> 01:02:36.000
- Bert Spencer:
Possibly, I don't know.

01:02:36.040 --> 01:02:37.480
If you jump on my
back, you'll get hurt.

01:02:37.560 --> 01:02:39.080
Okay?

01:02:39.160 --> 01:02:40.280
If you don't jump on my back,
I will love you

01:02:40.360 --> 01:02:43.080
and treat you out.

01:02:43.160 --> 01:02:46.400
Don't attack me,
because I am my daddy's boy,

01:02:46.440 --> 01:02:50.320
and it'll come out.

01:02:50.400 --> 01:02:51.400
Just not angry, you know.

01:02:51.480 --> 01:02:52.120
Is it?

01:02:52.200 --> 01:02:53.680
- David Wilson: No, it
feels like a threat.

01:02:53.720 --> 01:02:54.760
- Bert Spencer: Shut up.

01:02:54.840 --> 01:02:56.120
- David Wilson:
It does, it does.

01:02:56.200 --> 01:02:58.240
It feels like a threat, brother.

01:02:58.320 --> 01:02:59.200
- Bert Spencer: No, no.

01:02:59.280 --> 01:03:00.760
You asked me a question,
I'm trying to answer it.

01:03:00.840 --> 01:03:01.600
- David Wilson: Oh yeah.

01:03:01.680 --> 01:03:02.520
- Bert Spencer: Who am I?

01:03:02.560 --> 01:03:04.200
- David Wilson: You
answered it very clearly.

01:03:04.280 --> 01:03:04.880
- Bert Spencer: I did.

01:03:04.960 --> 01:03:05.760
- David Wilson: You did.

01:03:05.800 --> 01:03:06.520
- Bert Spencer: I meant it.

01:03:06.560 --> 01:03:08.040
- David Wilson: I understood
what you were arguing.

01:03:08.120 --> 01:03:09.400
- Bert Spencer: It's
not threatening you.

01:03:09.480 --> 01:03:12.160
It's not threatening anyone.

01:03:13.600 --> 01:03:21.360
If anyone jumps on my back
with aggression in mind,

01:03:21.440 --> 01:03:26.000
I can deal with it
like my dad did.

01:03:26.080 --> 01:03:27.400
I am my daddy's boy.

01:03:27.480 --> 01:03:28.960
- David Wilson: But
if we cross your back,

01:03:29.040 --> 01:03:31.280
if those people cross that--

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:32.880
- Bert Spencer: You can cross.

01:03:32.920 --> 01:03:35.960
I like it with you,
I want to hurt you.

01:03:36.040 --> 01:03:36.640
I like you.

01:03:36.720 --> 01:03:38.000
I'll question, I'll discuss.

01:03:38.080 --> 01:03:41.080
I wouldn't hurt
you; you've done it.

01:03:41.160 --> 01:03:42.200
You've crossed that barrier.

01:03:42.240 --> 01:03:44.800
I won't hurt you.

01:03:44.840 --> 01:03:47.400
I'll discuss with you.

01:03:47.440 --> 01:03:49.440
I'll question--I'll
question you.

01:03:49.520 --> 01:03:50.760
I'll question anybody.

01:03:50.840 --> 01:03:52.920
- David Wilson: What does
it mean to be labeled

01:03:53.000 --> 01:03:54.240
as a murderer?

01:03:54.280 --> 01:03:56.280
- Bert Spencer:
Just stop a minute.

01:04:02.480 --> 01:04:08.440
If you could switch off time
and go back in time, to 1978,

01:04:08.520 --> 01:04:14.440
yeah--1978, what would
you have changed from your

01:04:14.520 --> 01:04:15.640
life at that point?

01:04:15.720 --> 01:04:18.480
- Bert Spencer: I would
have been away on holiday.

01:04:18.560 --> 01:04:20.760
Away from the West Midlands.

01:04:20.800 --> 01:04:24.920
- David Wilson:
Bad memories in 78?

01:04:25.000 --> 01:04:31.760
- Bert Spencer: Stop.

01:04:31.840 --> 01:04:32.600
Bloody hell.

01:04:32.680 --> 01:04:34.080
- David Wilson: If you
want to stop, you can stop.

01:04:34.120 --> 01:04:34.760
- Bert Spencer: Just stop.

01:04:34.840 --> 01:04:37.880
- David Wilson: Yeah.

01:04:37.920 --> 01:04:39.680
It was a
truly remarkable day.

01:04:39.720 --> 01:04:44.360
I mean, the kinds of things
he's revealing in the course

01:04:46.680 --> 01:04:49.000
of the interview
are some of the things

01:04:49.040 --> 01:04:51.520
he's never
publicly stated before.

01:04:51.600 --> 01:04:57.880
He's told me that at the time
that he murdered Hubert Wilkes,

01:04:57.960 --> 01:04:59.840
he was having an affair.

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:03.720
And therefore, this whole
wife swapping alibi

01:05:03.800 --> 01:05:07.360
becomes more tenuous.

01:05:07.440 --> 01:05:10.680
You know, why do you care so
much about the woman that

01:05:10.720 --> 01:05:14.320
you've actually betrayed
by having an affair,

01:05:14.360 --> 01:05:18.360
and you say she was having an
affair at that time, as well.

01:05:18.400 --> 01:05:23.400
But the most remarkable thing
of all is the sense that

01:05:25.400 --> 01:05:27.800
he's putting
himself forward again.

01:05:27.880 --> 01:05:34.360
He's inserting himself into a
drama about his life

01:05:34.440 --> 01:05:39.480
and his role in the murder
of Carl Bridgewater.

01:05:39.560 --> 01:05:42.120
I mean, you know,
fair credit to him,

01:05:42.200 --> 01:05:47.320
I keep trying to explain to him
that people will look at this,

01:05:47.360 --> 01:05:49.640
and he calls
it trial by media.

01:05:49.720 --> 01:05:53.160
But actually, it's
him that's called.

01:05:53.240 --> 01:05:56.720
It's him that set
himself up as a cord.

01:05:56.800 --> 01:05:59.240
He's put himself in the dog.

01:05:59.320 --> 01:06:02.840
- James D'Arcy: As a former
prison governor, David Wilson,

01:06:02.920 --> 01:06:05.800
was trained to perform a
detailed checklist on prisoners

01:06:05.840 --> 01:06:08.840
he suspected of showing
signs of psychopathy.

01:06:08.920 --> 01:06:10.920
This is known as a P Scan.

01:06:11.000 --> 01:06:12.960
- David Wilson: What
I'll do tonight is,

01:06:13.040 --> 01:06:17.960
I'm going to do a P Scan on him,
which is simply looking to

01:06:18.040 --> 01:06:21.440
see if based on the things
that he's written,

01:06:21.520 --> 01:06:26.080
the performances in terms of
TV, my own knowledge now

01:06:26.160 --> 01:06:29.000
of him through some 20
hours of interview.

01:06:29.040 --> 01:06:33.160
Am I dealing with somebody
who's psychopathic?

01:06:33.200 --> 01:06:36.040
- James D'Arcy: Following
the last encounter,

01:06:36.120 --> 01:06:39.360
Bert calls the filmmakers to
tell them he is unhappy with

01:06:39.440 --> 01:06:41.520
the line of questioning.

01:06:41.600 --> 01:06:45.080
- Bert Spencer: I haven't
slept for two nights,

01:06:45.160 --> 01:06:45.760
thinking about it.

01:06:45.840 --> 01:06:48.240
You tell me to delve on
this and delve on that.

01:06:48.280 --> 01:06:51.560
I'm not happy with him,
he's all to do with multiple

01:06:51.640 --> 01:06:52.960
psychotic killers.

01:06:53.040 --> 01:06:54.960
Is he trying
to make me one?

01:06:55.040 --> 01:06:57.520
Is he trying to tie me
into Carl Bridgewater?

01:06:57.560 --> 01:06:59.960
And it feels
as though he is.

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:03.600
I wasn't there, I wasn't guilty,
and now he's raising it

01:07:03.640 --> 01:07:05.000
all up again.

01:07:05.080 --> 01:07:06.280
That criminalizes me.

01:07:06.360 --> 01:07:08.560
I can be bloody arrested.

01:07:08.600 --> 01:07:09.800
I am a lifer.

01:07:09.840 --> 01:07:12.720
He's got me
on trial by media.

01:07:12.800 --> 01:07:16.280
I want to ask him face to face,
"What are you out to achieve

01:07:16.320 --> 01:07:18.240
from this broadcast?"

01:07:18.280 --> 01:07:21.480
- James D'Arcy: Journalist
and writer, Simon Golding,

01:07:21.560 --> 01:07:24.000
has been working on
an account of Bert's life

01:07:24.080 --> 01:07:25.920
for the past two years.

01:07:26.000 --> 01:07:29.080
Simon introduced
David Wilson to Bert.

01:07:29.160 --> 01:07:31.960
As tensions mount following
the last interview,

01:07:32.040 --> 01:07:34.360
Bert has asked
Simon to visit him,

01:07:34.440 --> 01:07:36.880
to discuss the tone of
David Wilson's questioning.

01:07:36.960 --> 01:07:40.280
- Simon Golding: Professor
David Wilson will conduct

01:07:40.360 --> 01:07:45.040
his inquiry as he feels fit,
and he wants to be as

01:07:45.080 --> 01:07:47.640
open as possible,
and the only way I think

01:07:47.680 --> 01:07:50.400
Bert is going to really get something out of this,

01:07:50.480 --> 01:07:52.320
"Are we going
to move forward, "

01:07:52.400 --> 01:07:55.000
is if Bert is
open and truthful.

01:07:55.080 --> 01:08:01.920
Bert, you seemed quite concerned
about professor David Wilson

01:08:02.000 --> 01:08:05.840
and the interview, and how it's
actually going at the moment.

01:08:05.920 --> 01:08:10.240
And I, obviously, feel that you
are not very happy about it

01:08:10.320 --> 01:08:13.600
and the direction it's going
in, is that correct?

01:08:13.680 --> 01:08:15.200
- Bert Spencer: He's
a criminologist,

01:08:15.240 --> 01:08:16.480
and he's good at his job.

01:08:16.560 --> 01:08:22.960
Nice enough bloke, but he seems
to be--his role seems to be

01:08:23.040 --> 01:08:25.920
incriminating people,
whoever he's talking about

01:08:26.000 --> 01:08:30.040
and when he was talking to me,
I felt that there was

01:08:30.120 --> 01:08:33.160
some of that coming out in him, as though he was trying to

01:08:33.240 --> 01:08:36.160
incriminate me
without even knowing it.

01:08:36.240 --> 01:08:38.560
I've had enough of that, I've
had it for years, and years,

01:08:38.600 --> 01:08:39.400
I don't want anymore.

01:08:39.480 --> 01:08:42.840
- Simon Golding: Is it the
questioning or is it the--do you

01:08:42.920 --> 01:08:44.600
feel he's accusing you?

01:08:44.640 --> 01:08:47.400
Or what seems to be
the root problem?

01:08:47.440 --> 01:08:51.840
- Bert Spencer: One of the
things he brought up, okay,

01:08:51.920 --> 01:08:53.640
was I having an affair?

01:08:53.720 --> 01:08:55.800
- Bert Spencer: I
should have said, "Well,

01:08:55.880 --> 01:08:58.440
what's it got to do with you?"

01:08:58.480 --> 01:09:00.280
I said, "Yes.

01:09:00.360 --> 01:09:02.680
Yes, I had an affair, but
it wasn't really an affair,

01:09:02.720 --> 01:09:04.600
it was a fling.

01:09:04.680 --> 01:09:06.200
I won't give you
the person's name, "

01:09:06.280 --> 01:09:10.720
but that seemed to be a
stumbling block because if it

01:09:10.760 --> 01:09:15.760
happened to be my secretary,
at work, people would say,

01:09:15.800 --> 01:09:17.520
"She's covered up for him."

01:09:17.600 --> 01:09:19.560
It was not
my secretary at work.

01:09:19.600 --> 01:09:21.000
The police know that.

01:09:21.040 --> 01:09:23.320
They interviewed her, they
put her through a riddle.

01:09:23.400 --> 01:09:25.880
Microscopically,
in case it was her.

01:09:25.960 --> 01:09:27.360
No, it wasn't.

01:09:27.440 --> 01:09:29.680
We were just friends
and workmates.

01:09:29.720 --> 01:09:32.720
- Simon Golding: I think you
should use this opportunity

01:09:32.800 --> 01:09:38.320
for you to actually fire back,
like you are talking to me now,

01:09:38.400 --> 01:09:40.280
is to do that with
professor Wilson.

01:09:40.320 --> 01:09:41.720
- Bert Spencer: Right.

01:09:41.760 --> 01:09:44.440
- Simon Golding: And be open
and when you actually make

01:09:44.520 --> 01:09:47.280
those--make those points
because you're not

01:09:47.360 --> 01:09:50.440
on trial here,
you're just giving your chance,

01:09:50.520 --> 01:09:51.600
you know,
to mention something.

01:09:51.640 --> 01:09:53.160
- Bert Spencer: Yeah, okay,
but that's what I'd like.

01:09:53.200 --> 01:09:57.640
I'd like something else to, sort
of cement in people's minds

01:09:57.680 --> 01:09:59.960
that I am telling the truth.

01:10:00.040 --> 01:10:01.840
At the moment, it's
just me saying so.

01:10:01.880 --> 01:10:03.080
Okay?

01:10:03.120 --> 01:10:05.040
I happen to be a Quaker.

01:10:05.080 --> 01:10:09.080
I do tell the truth and one
point the public might like

01:10:09.120 --> 01:10:12.280
to concede their ears,
if ever I have to go to court as

01:10:12.320 --> 01:10:17.600
a witness for anything,
I don't have to take the oath.

01:10:17.680 --> 01:10:19.360
I'm a Quaker.

01:10:19.400 --> 01:10:21.840
I am expected
to tell the truth.

01:10:21.920 --> 01:10:25.440
Now, this--this phenomenon
that we're discussing

01:10:25.520 --> 01:10:28.360
is a major part of my life.

01:10:28.400 --> 01:10:32.000
It's not just something
silly, or minor.

01:10:32.040 --> 01:10:33.640
It's a major
part of my life.

01:10:33.720 --> 01:10:35.040
Very important to me.

01:10:35.120 --> 01:10:37.200
I have to tell the truth.

01:10:37.280 --> 01:10:40.880
And I'd like some--something
to corroborate that.

01:10:43.200 --> 01:10:46.520
♪ music playing ♪

01:10:46.600 --> 01:10:47.720
- James D'Arcy:
The last encounter between

01:10:47.800 --> 01:10:51.200
the two men ended with Bert
breaking down, however,

01:10:51.280 --> 01:10:55.640
Bert has invited David Wilson to
meet him again, but this time,

01:10:55.720 --> 01:11:02.600
in even more extraordinary
circumstances.

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:04.000
- David Wilson: Hi, Bert.

01:11:04.040 --> 01:11:05.280
- Bert Spencer:
Hello, again, David.

01:11:05.360 --> 01:11:06.320
- David Wilson:
Yeah, how are you?

01:11:06.400 --> 01:11:09.280
- James D'Arcy: The two
men are in Staffordshire.

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:12.200
It is the first time that Bert
has returned to the area since

01:11:12.280 --> 01:11:15.480
he murdered Hubert Wilkes.

01:11:15.560 --> 01:11:17.120
- Bert Spencer: We've
got a lot to do.

01:11:17.200 --> 01:11:18.200
- David Wilson: Yes.

01:11:18.280 --> 01:11:19.960
- Bert Spencer:
Let's get on then.

01:11:20.040 --> 01:11:21.880
- James D'Arcy: The first
place the two men visit is

01:11:21.920 --> 01:11:24.200
the cul-de-sac where
Bert used to live.

01:11:24.280 --> 01:11:26.160
A few doors away from
Carl Bridgewater.

01:11:30.120 --> 01:11:32.160
Bert lived as a neighbor
of the Bridgewater's

01:11:32.200 --> 01:11:34.440
for almost 8 years.

01:11:34.520 --> 01:11:38.080
Carl literally grew up on
the street where Bert lived.

01:11:38.160 --> 01:11:41.560
- Bert Spencer: Somewhere
here Carl lived.

01:11:41.640 --> 01:11:46.680
One of these houses and--we
lived in that one there.

01:11:46.720 --> 01:11:48.400
- David Wilson: Which one?

01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:51.800
- Bert Spencer: That
one there, look.

01:11:51.880 --> 01:11:53.080
- David Wilson: And Carl lived?

01:11:53.120 --> 01:11:54.200
- Bert: Somewhere there.

01:11:54.280 --> 01:11:57.000
- David Wilson: So,
one, two, three, four,

01:11:57.080 --> 01:11:59.920
five doors away is
Carl Bridgewater.

01:11:59.960 --> 01:12:00.720
- Bert Spencer: Yeah.

01:12:00.760 --> 01:12:01.600
Yeah.

01:12:01.680 --> 01:12:03.160
- David Wilson: Gosh,
I can't even imagine.

01:12:03.200 --> 01:12:05.200
See, this is why I am so
interested to be here.

01:12:05.280 --> 01:12:07.280
I can't have imagined it
was going to be really big.

01:12:07.360 --> 01:12:08.840
- Bert Spencer: No, this was it.

01:12:08.920 --> 01:12:10.200
- David Wilson:
It's very intimate.

01:12:10.240 --> 01:12:11.520
- Bert Spencer: This
was a kids' playground.

01:12:11.600 --> 01:12:13.040
- David Wilson: All the
kids play with each other?

01:12:13.120 --> 01:12:14.480
- Bert Spencer: Yeah, here.

01:12:14.560 --> 01:12:15.320
And my daughter.

01:12:15.400 --> 01:12:16.240
Yeah, here.

01:12:16.280 --> 01:12:17.200
- David Wilson: And Carl?

01:12:17.280 --> 01:12:19.120
- Bert Spencer: I don't know
because I didn't' play with him,

01:12:19.200 --> 01:12:20.240
I don't know who they were.

01:12:20.280 --> 01:12:21.000
- David Wilson: Yeah?

01:12:21.080 --> 01:12:23.120
- Bert: There were groups of
kids from all around this area,

01:12:23.200 --> 01:12:24.160
playing here.

01:12:24.240 --> 01:12:26.400
- David Wilson: But you have
no memory of Carl, well?

01:12:26.480 --> 01:12:29.320
- Bert Spencer: No, when I saw
Carl's picture in the paper,

01:12:29.360 --> 01:12:31.960
I thought, "Yeah, they
all look like that.

01:12:32.000 --> 01:12:35.160
They all--''I hope no one is
offended about the way I talk.

01:12:35.200 --> 01:12:36.400
I'm not offending anyone.

01:12:36.480 --> 01:12:38.440
It was just a kid
playing with other kids,

01:12:38.520 --> 01:12:40.800
it meant nothing to me.

01:12:40.840 --> 01:12:42.840
That's said in the
nicest possible way.

01:12:42.880 --> 01:12:44.040
I didn't know him.

01:12:44.120 --> 01:12:44.800
I didn't play with him.

01:12:44.840 --> 01:12:47.640
- David Wilson: And you
would move from here,

01:12:47.720 --> 01:12:53.520
when Carl was 10, and then he
was 13 when he was murdered.

01:12:53.600 --> 01:12:54.200
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

01:12:54.280 --> 01:12:54.880
I was--

01:12:54.920 --> 01:12:56.600
- David Wilson: So,
there are three years in between

01:12:56.680 --> 01:12:57.560
you living here and--

01:12:57.640 --> 01:12:59.600
- Bert Spencer:
And Kingsley Road.

01:12:59.680 --> 01:13:01.760
- David Wilson:
And Kingsley Road?

01:13:01.840 --> 01:13:02.480
- Bert Spencer: Yeah.

01:13:02.520 --> 01:13:03.960
It's lovely to come back,
it's--it's lovely to come back.

01:13:04.040 --> 01:13:07.320
You're flabbergasted.

01:13:07.360 --> 01:13:08.360
- David Wilson:
I'm flabbergasted.

01:13:08.400 --> 01:13:09.560
- Bert Spencer: Why?

01:13:09.600 --> 01:13:12.040
- David Wilson: Well,
I kind of thought this would

01:13:12.120 --> 01:13:14.160
be really emotional.

01:13:14.200 --> 01:13:15.000
- Bert Spencer: No, it isn't.

01:13:15.080 --> 01:13:18.040
It's lovely to come back
and see how nice this

01:13:18.080 --> 01:13:19.560
place has been made.

01:13:19.640 --> 01:13:23.520
- James D'Arcy: The next place
they visit is Yew Tree Farm

01:13:23.600 --> 01:13:27.880
where Carl Bridgewater
was murdered in 1978.

01:13:27.920 --> 01:13:31.200
Whilst he has always denied
involvement in Carl's murder,

01:13:31.280 --> 01:13:34.120
he has never denied that
he had been inside the farmhouse

01:13:34.200 --> 01:13:38.400
on at least three occasions, admired the antique furnishings

01:13:38.480 --> 01:13:41.080
and had also frequently
accessed the land for

01:13:41.160 --> 01:13:42.360
farming and shooting.

01:13:42.440 --> 01:13:44.640
- David Wilson: Oh,
gosh, this is it.

01:13:44.720 --> 01:13:45.320
Oh my gosh.

01:13:45.400 --> 01:13:49.880
- Bert Spencer: Look at this.

01:13:49.960 --> 01:13:53.960
Crikey.

01:13:54.040 --> 01:13:55.280
I was down here
hundreds of times.

01:13:55.360 --> 01:13:56.640
- David Wilson:
Hundreds of times.

01:13:56.680 --> 01:13:57.640
You knew the farm well.

01:13:57.680 --> 01:13:58.920
- Bert Spencer: Oh, crikey, yes.

01:13:59.000 --> 01:14:00.280
- David Wilson: And had you
been inside the farmhouse?

01:14:00.360 --> 01:14:01.480
- Bert Spencer:
Yes, I had, I had.

01:14:01.520 --> 01:14:02.520
- David Wilson: How many times?

01:14:02.600 --> 01:14:04.920
- Bert Spencer:
About two, three.

01:14:05.000 --> 01:14:07.160
I've been in, and I've
been in and spoke to them,

01:14:07.240 --> 01:14:08.520
and sat down with them.

01:14:08.560 --> 01:14:09.840
Cup of tea with them.

01:14:09.920 --> 01:14:11.760
Little old couple, very nice.

01:14:11.800 --> 01:14:13.800
- David Wilson: And the
couple that lived here,

01:14:13.880 --> 01:14:15.040
they were quite elderly.

01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:16.120
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

01:14:16.200 --> 01:14:17.800
They didn't go
out--they didn't go out.

01:14:17.840 --> 01:14:19.600
I knew people came; I
knew the doctor came.

01:14:19.680 --> 01:14:22.000
I knew someone delivered
papers, but not who.

01:14:22.040 --> 01:14:24.800
- David Wilson: Why did
they go out that day?

01:14:24.840 --> 01:14:28.320
- Bert Spencer: I don't know,
apparently--I don't really know.

01:14:28.400 --> 01:14:30.800
I think it was some
sort of treat for them.

01:14:30.880 --> 01:14:33.680
I don't really know that because
they didn't really go out.

01:14:33.720 --> 01:14:36.560
- David Wilson: Yeah, what's it
feel like to be standing outside

01:14:36.640 --> 01:14:38.600
the place that's--you know,
your name is attached.

01:14:38.680 --> 01:14:40.440
- Bert Spencer: I know.

01:14:40.520 --> 01:14:41.920
I know.

01:14:41.960 --> 01:14:42.880
- David Wilson: To the story of
the farm that once stood here

01:14:42.960 --> 01:14:43.960
- Bert Spencer: I know.

01:14:44.000 --> 01:14:49.600
It doesn't really feel strange,
I used to work up and down here.

01:14:49.640 --> 01:14:51.520
- David Wilson:
If Carl was alive,

01:14:51.600 --> 01:14:53.360
or you could speak
to Carl Bridgewater,

01:14:53.400 --> 01:14:55.400
what is it you would
like to say to him?

01:14:55.480 --> 01:14:58.640
- Bert Spencer: Well--
- David Wilson: Go on.

01:14:58.720 --> 01:15:02.760
- Bert Spencer: 35 years ago,
because of two horrific

01:15:02.840 --> 01:15:05.680
murder cases that happened
in this vicinity,

01:15:05.760 --> 01:15:08.280
your name and mine became
intrinsically joined.

01:15:10.320 --> 01:15:11.800
You are welcome
in my life, Carl,

01:15:11.880 --> 01:15:16.880
but how I wish--how I wish we
could've been fused together

01:15:19.160 --> 01:15:23.160
under different and
happier circumstances.

01:15:23.200 --> 01:15:24.600
Through the power
of silent prayer,

01:15:24.680 --> 01:15:29.640
I have spoken to and prayed
for you, Carl, many times.

01:15:33.440 --> 01:15:34.360
That's what I'd say to you.

01:15:34.440 --> 01:15:35.320
God bless you.

01:15:35.400 --> 01:15:37.400
- David Wilson: And what
do you think Carl might say

01:15:37.480 --> 01:15:38.080
back to you?

01:15:38.160 --> 01:15:38.920
- Bert Spencer: I don't know.

01:15:39.000 --> 01:15:40.520
I really don't.

01:15:40.600 --> 01:15:41.960
I don't know,
that's--I don't know,

01:15:42.040 --> 01:15:45.360
what do you think he might say?

01:15:45.440 --> 01:15:46.480
- David Wilson: He might
say you killed him.

01:15:46.520 --> 01:15:48.120
- Bert Spencer: He might,
but he would know that

01:15:48.200 --> 01:15:50.080
it would be a lie.

01:15:50.160 --> 01:15:51.160
He wouldn't say that.

01:15:51.240 --> 01:15:55.280
He was apparently a nice, little
boy scout, he wouldn't lie.

01:15:55.360 --> 01:15:57.880
- David Wilson: Why do you
think he got murdered that day?

01:15:57.960 --> 01:16:01.040
- Bert Spencer: Well, I can
only go on what the police said.

01:16:01.120 --> 01:16:03.280
It was a robbery and
it got disturbed.

01:16:03.360 --> 01:16:05.840
There was a robbery in progress.

01:16:05.880 --> 01:16:08.480
- David Wilson: Robbers don't
normally shoot 13-year-old boys.

01:16:08.560 --> 01:16:09.160
- Bert Spencer: No, no.

01:16:09.240 --> 01:16:10.600
Neither do ambulance
men, thank you very much.

01:16:10.680 --> 01:16:11.920
Point taken?

01:16:12.000 --> 01:16:14.400
- David Wilson: Ambulance
men don't normally shoot

01:16:14.480 --> 01:16:15.280
their best friends.

01:16:15.320 --> 01:16:17.040
- Bert Spencer: And
ambulance men, David,

01:16:17.120 --> 01:16:20.320
don't go on armed robberies in
full dress uniform to places

01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:21.440
where they are very well known.

01:16:21.520 --> 01:16:26.480
That's the point I
want you to understand.

01:16:26.560 --> 01:16:27.320
I wouldn't shoot a boy.

01:16:27.360 --> 01:16:29.440
There's no way I could even--

01:16:29.520 --> 01:16:32.280
- David Wilson: It's for me,
he was shot because

01:16:32.360 --> 01:16:34.280
he could
identify his killer.

01:16:34.360 --> 01:16:35.720
- Bert Spencer: That's
what the police say.

01:16:35.800 --> 01:16:38.400
- David Wilson: I would say,
I saw, looking at the evidence,

01:16:38.440 --> 01:16:39.240
I would say that.

01:16:39.320 --> 01:16:44.040
I would say, it's very unusual
for a robber to shoot a

01:16:44.080 --> 01:16:45.520
13-year-old boy.

01:16:45.600 --> 01:16:47.400
- Bert Spencer: I know what
you're thinking but let me just

01:16:47.480 --> 01:16:49.360
make this perfectly clear.

01:16:49.440 --> 01:16:52.960
I would not have, under
any circumstances,

01:16:53.040 --> 01:16:56.520
come in an armed robbery to a
farm I thought people were in

01:16:56.560 --> 01:16:59.360
and where I was known,
and I knew someone

01:16:59.400 --> 01:17:00.560
delivered papers now.

01:17:00.640 --> 01:17:03.160
- David Wilson: Maybe you
weren't doing an armed robbery.

01:17:03.200 --> 01:17:05.080
I'm not saying you were
doing an armed robbery;

01:17:05.160 --> 01:17:06.400
you could have been
doing anything.

01:17:06.480 --> 01:17:09.480
You could have been--you spotted
something when you were in the

01:17:09.560 --> 01:17:13.000
house, you liked those foxes,
you could've been taking things

01:17:13.040 --> 01:17:15.040
that--to sell on.

01:17:16.400 --> 01:17:19.160
And Carl Bridgewater, because
the back door was open,

01:17:19.240 --> 01:17:22.040
was used to walking in if
the back door was open because

01:17:22.080 --> 01:17:24.960
he would hand deliver the
papers to the elderly couple.

01:17:25.040 --> 01:17:26.320
That's what is in
the police statement.

01:17:26.400 --> 01:17:29.840
So, if he sees the back
door open, he walks in,

01:17:29.920 --> 01:17:33.160
and he then encounters
somebody, and he goes,

01:17:33.200 --> 01:17:35.360
"Oh, hello Mr. Spencer."

01:17:35.400 --> 01:17:38.440
- Bert Spencer: Yeah, that's
the theory of yours, is it?

01:17:38.520 --> 01:17:40.480
- David Wilson: No, it's
actually--a theory that's

01:17:40.560 --> 01:17:41.880
in a lot--

01:17:41.960 --> 01:17:43.240
- Bert Spencer:
It's a police theory.

01:17:43.320 --> 01:17:45.400
Do you believe the police?

01:17:45.440 --> 01:17:46.200
The lies they've
talked about me.

01:17:46.240 --> 01:17:47.440
And you still believe them.

01:17:47.480 --> 01:17:49.440
- David Wilson: I
think that he was shot,

01:17:49.520 --> 01:17:53.400
Carl was shot because he could
identify the person or killer--

01:17:53.480 --> 01:17:54.680
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

01:17:54.760 --> 01:17:56.520
- David Wilson: Who
was breaking in.

01:17:56.600 --> 01:17:57.920
- Bert Spencer: That's feasible.

01:17:58.000 --> 01:17:58.720
- David Wilson:
Is that feasible?

01:17:58.800 --> 01:17:59.840
- Bert: It's feasible, I
can't argue against that,

01:17:59.920 --> 01:18:02.320
but who were the people?

01:18:02.400 --> 01:18:05.560
It certainly wasn't me.

01:18:05.600 --> 01:18:10.600
The four who were arrested for
it, I believed had done it.

01:18:10.680 --> 01:18:13.080
I don't know, I
believed they did it.

01:18:13.160 --> 01:18:14.840
- David Wilson: Do you
still believe they did it?

01:18:14.920 --> 01:18:16.000
- Bert Spencer: Yes, I do.

01:18:16.040 --> 01:18:18.160
Yes, I do.

01:18:18.240 --> 01:18:20.520
So, I don't give a damn what
they protested and how many

01:18:20.600 --> 01:18:22.640
rooftops they sat on.

01:18:22.720 --> 01:18:25.640
It didn't affect me or
my judgement of them.

01:18:25.680 --> 01:18:30.640
I believed they and their group
were deliberately trying to find

01:18:30.720 --> 01:18:32.960
a scapegoat and I fit it.

01:18:33.040 --> 01:18:33.880
That's what I believe.

01:18:33.920 --> 01:18:34.520
I won't change.

01:18:34.600 --> 01:18:37.880
If that upsets you, tough.

01:18:37.960 --> 01:18:40.720
- David Wilson: The evidence
against them was thrown

01:18:40.760 --> 01:18:42.360
out eventually.

01:18:42.440 --> 01:18:43.320
- Bert Spencer: Yes, yeah.

01:18:43.400 --> 01:18:44.880
- David Wilson: So--

01:18:44.960 --> 01:18:47.080
- Bert Spencer: The same damn cops that caused

01:18:47.160 --> 01:18:49.120
that handled my case.

01:18:49.200 --> 01:18:50.360
They might throw mine out.

01:18:50.440 --> 01:18:51.280
Why not?

01:18:51.360 --> 01:18:54.960
♪ music playing ♪

01:18:55.040 --> 01:18:56.520
- James D'Arcy: The Bridgewater Four would find themselves

01:18:56.600 --> 01:18:58.640
at the heart of one
of the greatest miscarriages

01:18:58.680 --> 01:19:02.280
of justice in British
legal history.

01:19:02.320 --> 01:19:04.800
Their convictions were
eventually quashed when it

01:19:04.880 --> 01:19:08.280
was proven the detectives
had faked a confession.

01:19:08.320 --> 01:19:15.200
11 years after Carl's murder,
the West Midland serious crime

01:19:15.280 --> 01:19:19.440
squad was disbanded following
accusations of abuses of power.

01:19:19.520 --> 01:19:25.040
Their mishandling of the case
meant that other leads that

01:19:25.080 --> 01:19:28.640
might have been investigated
were never properly followed up.

01:19:28.680 --> 01:19:32.600
This included the identity of a
suspect in a photo fit picture

01:19:32.680 --> 01:19:35.320
who had been seen near Yew
Tree Farm on the day that Carl

01:19:35.400 --> 01:19:37.440
Bridgewater was murdered.

01:19:37.520 --> 01:19:42.720
- David Wilson: Here is
the photo fit of one of

01:19:42.760 --> 01:19:44.680
the men that was seen that day.

01:19:44.760 --> 01:19:49.560
Yeah, and above
is Hubert Wilkes.

01:19:49.600 --> 01:19:53.040
- Bert: It should be
more squint, more jawing.

01:19:53.120 --> 01:19:54.880
It doesn't look like him.

01:19:54.960 --> 01:19:56.200
- David Wilson: You don't
think that looks like him?

01:19:56.280 --> 01:19:57.160
- Bert Spencer: No, no.

01:19:57.240 --> 01:19:59.840
He had no hair like that,
and he had squinty eyes.

01:19:59.920 --> 01:20:00.840
He was jawly.

01:20:00.920 --> 01:20:01.840
You know what I mean by that.

01:20:01.920 --> 01:20:03.280
- David Wilson: But
that looks uncanny.

01:20:03.360 --> 01:20:06.760
You don't think he could've
been at Yew Tree Farm back then?

01:20:06.840 --> 01:20:10.200
- Bert Spencer: I
never thought that.

01:20:10.280 --> 01:20:11.680
- David Wilson: Is
that a possibility?

01:20:11.720 --> 01:20:14.360
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

01:20:14.440 --> 01:20:16.680
He lived there.

01:20:16.760 --> 01:20:18.800
He got right to be here.

01:20:18.880 --> 01:20:19.680
He rented the farm.

01:20:19.760 --> 01:20:22.960
- David Wilson: He was
a father figure to you.

01:20:23.000 --> 01:20:23.760
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

01:20:23.800 --> 01:20:25.560
- David Wilson: He's
someone you talk to a lot.

01:20:25.600 --> 01:20:28.120
He's someone that you discuss
these things with a lot.

01:20:28.200 --> 01:20:31.040
Did he never mention
the murder at the farm?

01:20:31.080 --> 01:20:32.040
- Bert Spencer: No, no.

01:20:32.120 --> 01:20:34.640
I didn't know he was
also a main suspect.

01:20:34.720 --> 01:20:35.840
I didn't know.

01:20:35.880 --> 01:20:39.640
I didn't know that someone
had seen someone they believed

01:20:39.680 --> 01:20:40.680
was his vehicle.

01:20:40.760 --> 01:20:44.720
Not just a Land Rover,
someone believed it was his,

01:20:44.760 --> 01:20:45.360
someone local.

01:20:45.440 --> 01:20:48.480
- James D'Arcy: Local
farmworker, Ian Charles,

01:20:48.520 --> 01:20:51.160
was working in the fields
next to Yew Tree Farm on

01:20:51.240 --> 01:20:53.400
the day Carl was murdered.

01:20:53.480 --> 01:20:57.000
In his statement to police, he
reported that he saw what he

01:20:57.040 --> 01:20:59.840
thought was Hubert Wilkes'
Land Rover parked in

01:20:59.920 --> 01:21:01.720
the driveway
of Yew Tree Farm.

01:21:01.800 --> 01:21:04.040
- Ian Charles: "At the
time I saw the Land Rover,

01:21:04.080 --> 01:21:07.720
I thought that Mr. Wilkes or
one of his sons or laborers was

01:21:07.800 --> 01:21:08.920
visiting the farm."

01:21:09.000 --> 01:21:10.560
- James D'Arcy:
Wilkes had already been

01:21:10.640 --> 01:21:11.680
questioned by police.

01:21:11.720 --> 01:21:16.520
Telling them he had driven his
green BMW to market on that day,

01:21:16.600 --> 01:21:20.000
but the day after the farmworker
had spoken to the police,

01:21:20.080 --> 01:21:22.520
Wilkes changed a number of
things in his testimony.

01:21:22.600 --> 01:21:26.600
Including that he had actually
been driving his Land Rover

01:21:26.680 --> 01:21:28.560
on that day.

01:21:28.600 --> 01:21:32.400
A persistent rumor was that Bert
killed Wilkes because the farmer

01:21:32.440 --> 01:21:35.720
had seen him at Yew Tree Farm,
then taunted him about his

01:21:35.800 --> 01:21:37.600
alleged involvement
in Carl's death.

01:21:37.640 --> 01:21:42.880
Bert has always denied this,
but a letter found amongst Anne

01:21:42.960 --> 01:21:46.680
Wheelan's papers states that
Carl's death was indeed being

01:21:46.760 --> 01:21:48.920
discussed the night
Wilkes was shot dead.

01:21:51.720 --> 01:21:54.560
- "We can confirm that Mrs.
Spencer told our reporter,

01:21:54.640 --> 01:21:57.960
Mr. Tony Bishop, that there
had been talk about the Carl

01:21:58.040 --> 01:22:01.520
Bridgewater case at Holloway
House Farm on the evening that

01:22:01.560 --> 01:22:02.880
Mr. Wilkes was shot."

01:22:02.960 --> 01:22:04.520
- James D'Arcy:
But in the end,

01:22:04.600 --> 01:22:07.480
she was satisfied that her
husband has no involvement in

01:22:07.560 --> 01:22:09.200
the Carl Bridgewater affair.

01:22:09.280 --> 01:22:12.960
- David Wilson: Did Hubert
Wilkes ever mention robberies?

01:22:13.040 --> 01:22:15.480
- Bert Spencer: No.

01:22:15.560 --> 01:22:20.720
- David Wilson: Did he never
say to you in your house,

01:22:20.800 --> 01:22:23.520
"You can do a robbery and
escape through the woods?"

01:22:23.560 --> 01:22:25.400
- Bert Spencer: Oh, no!

01:22:25.480 --> 01:22:26.040
Don't be silly.

01:22:26.120 --> 01:22:26.880
You're being silly, David.

01:22:26.920 --> 01:22:28.000
No.

01:22:28.080 --> 01:22:31.080
- David Wilson: I got that
directly from your daughter.

01:22:31.160 --> 01:22:34.640
- Bert Spencer's daughter:
I never liked him.

01:22:34.720 --> 01:22:39.200
When he came to our house,
I particularly remember

01:22:39.280 --> 01:22:43.400
he would stand at
the fire with his back

01:22:43.440 --> 01:22:47.240
to the fire
and I was lying in the sitting.

01:22:47.280 --> 01:22:51.600
He would just constantly talk
about robberies and how you

01:22:51.680 --> 01:22:56.960
could commit the perfect crime
by escaping through the woods.

01:22:57.040 --> 01:23:01.920
And I'd lie there and
think, "Oh, just shut up!"

01:23:02.640 --> 01:23:05.240
Because I never
particularly liked the man.

01:23:05.320 --> 01:23:10.880
I never felt any
liking towards him.

01:23:10.960 --> 01:23:13.040
- James D'Arcy: Holloway House
Farm is the final stop of the

01:23:13.120 --> 01:23:14.680
day, Bert's life changed forever
when he murdered Hubert Wilkes.

01:23:14.760 --> 01:23:17.360
In the minds of some, it was
events surrounding that murder

01:23:17.440 --> 01:23:24.560
that still inextricably
link him to the death

01:23:24.640 --> 01:23:27.280
of Carl Bridgewater.

01:23:27.360 --> 01:23:30.840
- David Wilson: Farmer Wilkes
farmed Yew Tree Farm as well.

01:23:30.920 --> 01:23:33.160
- Bert Spencer: And this farm,
that's Holloway

01:23:33.240 --> 01:23:35.200
House Farm there.

01:23:35.280 --> 01:23:36.000
This is as it was.

01:23:36.080 --> 01:23:37.680
- David Wilson: Oh my gosh,
it looks pretty impressive,

01:23:37.760 --> 01:23:38.200
doesn't it?

01:23:38.280 --> 01:23:41.000
- Bert Spencer: That is how it
was, and I was his gardener,

01:23:41.080 --> 01:23:43.320
his chauffeur--

01:23:43.400 --> 01:23:46.400
- David Wilson: Bert,
you wanted to say something

01:23:46.480 --> 01:23:48.520
to Carl Bridgewater
at Yew Tree Farm.

01:23:48.600 --> 01:23:50.840
What would you say
to Hubert Wilkes?

01:23:50.920 --> 01:23:53.080
- Bert Spencer: I am so
ashamed, I'll say nothing.

01:23:53.160 --> 01:23:54.040
- David Wilson: Nothing?

01:23:54.080 --> 01:23:55.280
- Bert Spencer: No, this hurts.

01:23:55.360 --> 01:24:02.040
This hurts because I
shot him, didn't I?

01:24:02.080 --> 01:24:03.560
A friend.

01:24:03.640 --> 01:24:05.840
It hurts, it hurts me.

01:24:05.920 --> 01:24:09.840
I don't expect you to
understand that it does.

01:24:09.920 --> 01:24:11.320
- David Wilson: No,
I understand it.

01:24:11.360 --> 01:24:14.200
I understand that it's very
emotional and overpowering.

01:24:14.280 --> 01:24:16.040
- Bert Spencer: As much as
I went bloody mad, didn't I?

01:24:16.120 --> 01:24:18.080
For a few minutes.

01:24:18.160 --> 01:24:22.800
- David Wilson: And again,
some of the things that

01:24:22.840 --> 01:24:27.440
I would want to ask of you,
how come Hubert Wilkes ends up

01:24:27.520 --> 01:24:30.200
dead in exactly the same
way asCarl Bridgewater?

01:24:30.280 --> 01:24:31.920
- Bert Spencer: I
don't know about that.

01:24:32.000 --> 01:24:35.680
I don't know the circumstances,
or what do you call it?

01:24:35.760 --> 01:24:37.600
Coincidence or whatever.

01:24:37.680 --> 01:24:40.680
But apparently, it did.

01:24:40.720 --> 01:24:43.000
- David Wilson: Yeah,
that must strike you.

01:24:43.080 --> 01:24:45.960
That coincidence must
strike you as eerie and odd.

01:24:46.040 --> 01:24:47.400
- Bert Spencer: Yes, yes.

01:24:47.480 --> 01:24:49.600
They said to me--the
police said to me,

01:24:49.680 --> 01:24:51.200
it's exactly the
way as Carl died.

01:24:51.240 --> 01:24:52.440
Exactly the same.

01:24:53.720 --> 01:24:56.160
- James D'Arcy: Next time
on Interview with a Murderer,

01:24:56.200 --> 01:24:59.400
Bert stuns professor Wilson by
calling in on his secretary

01:24:59.440 --> 01:25:01.440
and cast iron alibi, Barbara.

01:25:01.520 --> 01:25:03.040
- Bert Spencer:
Barbara lived up here.

01:25:03.080 --> 01:25:04.760
- James D'Arcy:
But after 36 years,

01:25:04.840 --> 01:25:07.400
can she still back up his
story that he was at work

01:25:07.480 --> 01:25:08.840
all day when Carl was shot?

01:25:08.880 --> 01:25:10.760
- David Wilson: Can you
remember if Bert took lunch

01:25:10.840 --> 01:25:13.440
that day that Carl
Bridgewater was murdered?

01:25:13.520 --> 01:25:16.720
- James D'Arcy: And breaking
nearly a four decade long

01:25:16.760 --> 01:25:20.480
silence, a crucial new
witness comes forward.

01:25:20.560 --> 01:25:22.280
- David Wilson: Somebody
has made contact with us,

01:25:22.360 --> 01:25:25.480
that I've been wanting
to speak to throughout

01:25:25.560 --> 01:25:27.800
this entire process.

01:25:27.880 --> 01:25:33.880
Bert's first wife, Janette has
never spoken before on camera.

01:25:33.960 --> 01:25:36.400
I don't know what
she's going to say,

01:25:36.440 --> 01:25:39.760
but I know that whatever
she says is going to

01:25:39.840 --> 01:25:42.240
be absolutely fascinating.

01:25:43.280 --> 01:25:45.120
- David Wilson: What does
it mean to be labeled as a

01:25:45.200 --> 01:25:46.200
murderer?

01:25:46.280 --> 01:25:48.840
- Bert Spencer:
Just stop a minute.

01:25:48.880 --> 01:25:52.160
- James D'Arcy: Could
farmer Hubert Wilkes,

01:25:52.240 --> 01:25:55.640
who Bert murdered, have been a
witness at Yew Tree Farm the day

01:25:55.720 --> 01:25:57.120
Carl died?

01:25:57.160 --> 01:26:00.960
- David Wilson: You don't think
he could've been at Yew Tree

01:26:01.040 --> 01:26:02.400
Farm that day?

01:26:02.480 --> 01:26:04.680
Is that a possibility?

01:26:04.760 --> 01:26:06.560
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

01:26:06.600 --> 01:26:08.600
- James D'Arcy: And professor
Wilson fears that Bert may be

01:26:08.680 --> 01:26:11.040
displaying some worrying
personality traits.

01:26:11.120 --> 01:26:12.760
- David Wilson: Am I
dealing with somebody who is

01:26:12.840 --> 01:26:13.760
psychopathic?

01:26:13.840 --> 01:26:17.440
- James D'Arcy: For all
the suspicions about Bert,

01:26:17.480 --> 01:26:20.360
there has always been one person
who has supported his claim that

01:26:20.440 --> 01:26:22.960
he had nothing to do
with the murder of Carl.

01:26:23.040 --> 01:26:25.680
His secretary and cast
iron alibi, Barbara.

01:26:25.720 --> 01:26:29.760
Barbara has refused all
requests for interviews in the

01:26:29.800 --> 01:26:33.080
intervening years, but Bert has
returned to Staffordshire with

01:26:33.160 --> 01:26:36.720
criminologist David Wilson, in
a bid to once and for all prove

01:26:36.800 --> 01:26:39.600
that he had nothing to
do with Carl's death.

01:26:39.680 --> 01:26:42.960
During the trip, Bert stuns
everyone by asking the car to

01:26:43.040 --> 01:26:45.520
stop outside Barbara's home.

01:26:45.600 --> 01:26:48.480
- Bert Spencer:
Barbara lived up here.

01:26:48.560 --> 01:26:50.760
Oh, we got a camera!

01:26:51.120 --> 01:26:52.160
Hello, camera.

01:26:57.560 --> 01:26:58.800
Oh, I got the wrong place,
does Barbara live here?

01:26:58.880 --> 01:27:00.840
- Julie; Who wants to know?

01:27:00.920 --> 01:27:01.720
- Bert Spencer: Are you Julie?

01:27:01.760 --> 01:27:03.520
I'm Bert.

01:27:03.560 --> 01:27:06.360
I'm Bert.

01:27:06.400 --> 01:27:09.000
Come here!

01:27:09.080 --> 01:27:14.120
Come here, [INDISCERNIBLE]
- Julie: Oh my God!

01:27:14.200 --> 01:27:16.440
- Bert Spencer: All those years.

01:27:16.520 --> 01:27:17.920
You look lovely.

01:27:18.000 --> 01:27:18.760
Is she in?

01:27:18.840 --> 01:27:22.440
Is she alright?

01:27:22.480 --> 01:27:23.200
Barbara!

01:27:23.280 --> 01:27:24.720
- Barbara: Bert!

01:27:24.800 --> 01:27:26.880
- Bert Spencer: Hey, babe.

01:27:26.960 --> 01:27:27.640
Look at you.

01:27:27.680 --> 01:27:29.120
- Barbara: I'm going to cry.

01:27:29.200 --> 01:27:31.200
- Bert Spencer:
Let's cry together.

01:27:31.280 --> 01:27:33.560
Come here.

01:27:33.640 --> 01:27:36.160
- Barbara: We're not going
through all this again, are we?

01:27:36.200 --> 01:27:36.760
- Bert Spencer: No, no.

01:27:36.800 --> 01:27:38.120
It's better this time, lovey.

01:27:38.160 --> 01:27:39.640
Better this time.

01:27:39.720 --> 01:27:40.880
I wrote a book, Barbara.

01:27:40.960 --> 01:27:43.600
- Barbara: Yeah.

01:27:43.680 --> 01:27:45.560
- Bert Spencer: To show why a
lot of people were wrong in the

01:27:45.600 --> 01:27:46.760
allegations.

01:27:46.800 --> 01:27:48.800
I'm bloody crying.

01:27:48.840 --> 01:27:55.400
And because of the
book--come here, you.

01:27:55.440 --> 01:27:57.240
- David Wilson: What's
it like seeing him again?

01:27:57.280 --> 01:27:59.520
- Barbara: I can't believe it.

01:27:59.600 --> 01:28:00.880
- Bert Spencer: It's
a long, long time.

01:28:00.960 --> 01:28:03.040
You still look lovely.

01:28:03.120 --> 01:28:04.320
Jump on it, my life.

01:28:04.400 --> 01:28:07.840
- David Wilson: Answer me this
question and I want Bert not to

01:28:07.920 --> 01:28:09.440
interrupt.

01:28:09.520 --> 01:28:10.680
- Bert Spencer: Get on with it.

01:28:10.760 --> 01:28:11.640
- David Wilson: Why?

01:28:11.720 --> 01:28:13.960
The one thing that has always
confused me about the Colbert

01:28:14.000 --> 01:28:17.840
Hospital part of the story
are the records disappearing.

01:28:17.920 --> 01:28:19.600
Why do you think
they disappeared?

01:28:19.680 --> 01:28:23.560
- Barbara: Well, you see--it was
an open plan and very peculiar

01:28:23.640 --> 01:28:26.040
people went there at night.

01:28:26.080 --> 01:28:27.160
Didn't they?

01:28:27.240 --> 01:28:29.560
- Bert Spencer: They used to
steal computers and tele file in

01:28:29.640 --> 01:28:31.400
the carpets.

01:28:31.480 --> 01:28:33.240
Sorry, to interfere.

01:28:33.280 --> 01:28:35.080
- David Wilson: I never knew I
was going to get a chance to say

01:28:35.120 --> 01:28:39.160
hello to you and the one bit
from your witness statements is,

01:28:39.240 --> 01:28:43.880
I wonder if we can clarify, is
that you didn't know if perhaps

01:28:43.960 --> 01:28:48.720
Bert had gone for lunch on the
day that Carl Bridgewater had

01:28:48.760 --> 01:28:49.360
been murdered.

01:28:49.440 --> 01:28:52.000
You didn't know--he could
have gone for lunch, you said,

01:28:52.040 --> 01:28:55.200
and he normally takes his lunch
between 12:30, could go on,

01:28:55.240 --> 01:28:57.160
perhaps as late as, 2.

01:28:57.240 --> 01:28:59.760
Can you remember if Bert
took lunch that day that Carl

01:28:59.840 --> 01:29:04.040
Bridgewater was murdered?

01:29:04.080 --> 01:29:06.240
- Barbara: That's
[INDISCERNIBLE] going back a few

01:29:06.280 --> 01:29:11.080
years, I cannot remember what
exactly I said with time,

01:29:11.120 --> 01:29:17.640
but this is--I always
used to go from 1-1:30.

01:29:17.720 --> 01:29:24.400
At various times, he's be--you
got lots of hospitals to count

01:29:24.440 --> 01:29:25.040
with.

01:29:25.120 --> 01:29:26.680
You can't count just
with just Colbert.

01:29:26.760 --> 01:29:29.440
- David Wilson: So, he could
have gone for lunch on that day

01:29:29.520 --> 01:29:31.760
between those times.

01:29:31.840 --> 01:29:32.440
- Barbara: Yes.

01:29:32.480 --> 01:29:32.960
- David Wilson: Yes.

01:29:33.040 --> 01:29:33.600
Well, that's great!

01:29:33.640 --> 01:29:36.520
That's what you said in your
statement and I just wanted to

01:29:36.600 --> 01:29:38.200
clarify that and
that's wonderful.

01:29:38.280 --> 01:29:39.760
Thank you.

01:29:39.840 --> 01:29:44.880
You provide Bert with an
alibi because you say, "No,

01:29:44.920 --> 01:29:48.160
he was at Colbert Hospital,"
and then there's this bit in the

01:29:48.240 --> 01:29:51.360
statement that you make, and
you say, "Well, I take my lunch,

01:29:51.440 --> 01:29:55.680
and he could have also taken
his lunch and therefore,

01:29:55.760 --> 01:29:56.760
I didn't see him.

01:29:56.800 --> 01:29:58.120
It may have been an
open-plan office,

01:29:58.200 --> 01:30:02.160
but I didn't see him
continuously during the day,

01:30:02.200 --> 01:30:05.160
and he would have disappeared to
go for lunch." - Barbara: Yeah.

01:30:05.240 --> 01:30:07.520
- David Wilson: And that's
a possibility, isn't it?

01:30:07.600 --> 01:30:08.200
So.

01:30:08.240 --> 01:30:12.320
That's the bit of a story that
I'm trying to make sense of.

01:30:12.400 --> 01:30:14.080
- Bert Spencer: I might
or I might--I don't know.

01:30:14.120 --> 01:30:15.680
I might or might not
have gone for lunch.

01:30:15.720 --> 01:30:17.800
I'm just going up for
dinner upstairs, again,

01:30:17.880 --> 01:30:21.960
to eat sometimes, but it doesn't
matter because Carl was killed

01:30:22.040 --> 01:30:27.480
around 4 something and after
someone saw a blue Viva go into

01:30:27.560 --> 01:30:30.800
the farm drive, a quarter
of an hour afterwards,

01:30:30.880 --> 01:30:33.800
there were other vehicles who
didn't see the blue Vauxhall

01:30:33.880 --> 01:30:34.480
Viva.

01:30:34.560 --> 01:30:36.480
So, it had gone in and gone out.

01:30:36.560 --> 01:30:39.440
Long before Carl was killed.

01:30:39.520 --> 01:30:42.080
- David Wilson: I wonder if it
became so frightening for you,

01:30:42.160 --> 01:30:42.720
Bert.

01:30:42.800 --> 01:30:43.800
- Bert Spencer: I'm not
frightened of anything;

01:30:43.880 --> 01:30:44.480
you should know that.

01:30:44.560 --> 01:30:46.520
- David Wilson: I wonder if it
became so frightening for you,

01:30:46.600 --> 01:30:49.440
that if you admitted it was
you going for your lunch,

01:30:49.480 --> 01:30:52.120
and you turned around
because then people would go,

01:30:52.200 --> 01:30:54.960
"Wait a minute," they've already
had the fact that you collected

01:30:55.040 --> 01:30:58.000
antiques and "I went for my
lunch and turned my car."

01:30:58.080 --> 01:30:59.040
- Bert Spencer: Let me come in.

01:30:59.120 --> 01:31:00.960
I could've said if I were there.

01:31:01.000 --> 01:31:03.840
I was there, I had
every right to be there.

01:31:03.920 --> 01:31:05.560
Anytime, day or night.

01:31:05.600 --> 01:31:07.000
I worked down the fields.

01:31:07.080 --> 01:31:08.360
I knew the people.

01:31:08.400 --> 01:31:10.400
I worked for the farmer
who had the place.

01:31:10.440 --> 01:31:14.040
I had a right to be there, but
it wasn't my car on that day.

01:31:14.120 --> 01:31:18.160
- David Wilson: That part of
the story, the Vauxhall Viva,

01:31:18.240 --> 01:31:19.080
connects you.

01:31:19.120 --> 01:31:22.240
Whether you like it
or not, to this story.

01:31:22.280 --> 01:31:23.320
- Bert Spencer: Right.

01:31:23.360 --> 01:31:25.000
Now, whether you like
it or not, David,

01:31:25.080 --> 01:31:27.520
if I had gone home
for lunch on that day,

01:31:27.600 --> 01:31:29.720
I did not pass Yew Tree Farm.

01:31:29.760 --> 01:31:32.000
- David Wilson: So, did you
go for lunch on that day?

01:31:32.080 --> 01:31:33.600
- Bert Spencer: I don't
know, for Christ's sake.

01:31:33.680 --> 01:31:35.960
- David Wilson: You don't know!

01:31:36.000 --> 01:31:38.960
You have--this is, this is
the story that you've been the

01:31:39.000 --> 01:31:41.760
scapegoat for murder, and you
can't remember if you went home

01:31:41.800 --> 01:31:44.760
for lunch on that day.

01:31:44.840 --> 01:31:45.560
- Bert Spencer: No, I didn't.

01:31:45.640 --> 01:31:46.680
- Barbara: He didn't
have a set pattern.

01:31:46.760 --> 01:31:47.960
- Bert Spencer: I didn't
have a set pattern, David.

01:31:48.040 --> 01:31:51.520
I was all over the bloody place
and my phones never stopped,

01:31:51.600 --> 01:31:53.080
and I was up to--
- David Wilson: So,

01:31:53.120 --> 01:31:55.640
if you had no set pattern, Bert,
you could've been there at 4

01:31:55.680 --> 01:31:58.520
o'clock, you could have
been there at 5 o'clock.

01:31:58.560 --> 01:32:02.680
By ruling--by opening up
for other possibilities,

01:32:02.720 --> 01:32:06.680
you make it more complicated
and more possible.

01:32:06.760 --> 01:32:08.000
- Bert Spencer: Let's
close those possibilities.

01:32:08.080 --> 01:32:11.280
Let's close those possibilities
that you're making in your mind.

01:32:11.360 --> 01:32:12.600
- David Wilson: I'm not
making them in my mind.

01:32:12.640 --> 01:32:14.480
- Bert Spencer: Will you listen?

01:32:14.560 --> 01:32:15.200
- David Wilson: Sure.

01:32:15.280 --> 01:32:17.120
- Bert Spencer: I didn't
go home for lunch.

01:32:17.200 --> 01:32:19.280
You'll have to believe
me; you can't disprove it.

01:32:19.360 --> 01:32:21.040
- David Wilson: I don't want
us to argue any further.

01:32:21.080 --> 01:32:22.560
- Bert Spencer:
We're not arguing,

01:32:22.640 --> 01:32:24.800
we're seeing as discussing
something that touches my

01:32:24.880 --> 01:32:25.480
emotions.

01:32:25.520 --> 01:32:26.600
I've clarified that.

01:32:26.680 --> 01:32:28.680
- David Wilson: Good, I
am pleased about that.

01:32:28.720 --> 01:32:30.120
I am pleased that you've
got good memories,

01:32:30.160 --> 01:32:36.200
and I'm delighted to
have met you, Barbara.

01:32:38.560 --> 01:32:40.520
(music playing)

01:32:40.560 --> 01:32:42.880
- James D'Arcy: Bert's claim
that Barbara represents a cast

01:32:42.960 --> 01:32:45.840
iron alibi no longer stands up.

01:32:47.200 --> 01:33:01.320
(music playing)

01:33:01.400 --> 01:33:03.880
Following the astonishing
revelation by Barbara,

01:33:03.920 --> 01:33:07.920
David Wilson, reassembles
his team of researchers.

01:33:07.960 --> 01:33:11.160
He feels he is not ready to
present his theory about the

01:33:11.200 --> 01:33:14.240
involvement Bert may
have had in Carl's death.

01:33:14.320 --> 01:33:19.400
- David Wilson: One of the
things that confuses about

01:33:19.440 --> 01:33:23.480
Bert's relationship to the
murder at Yew Tree Farm and Carl

01:33:23.560 --> 01:33:27.880
Bridgewater has always been that
he had an alibi supported by

01:33:27.960 --> 01:33:28.880
Barbara.

01:33:28.960 --> 01:33:34.440
Bert says he was at Colbert
Hospital all day on the day that

01:33:34.480 --> 01:33:39.520
Carl is murdered and then, of
course, I've been with her,

01:33:39.560 --> 01:33:42.920
with Bert Spencer, almost
four decades later,

01:33:43.000 --> 01:33:46.960
and I've listened to what
she's had to say and frankly,

01:33:47.040 --> 01:33:52.680
what she has to say is that he
has no alibi on that particular

01:33:52.760 --> 01:33:54.600
day at all.

01:33:54.640 --> 01:33:57.000
We have to put him
under pressure,

01:33:57.040 --> 01:34:02.720
either to admit he knows
something more about Yew Tree

01:34:02.800 --> 01:34:03.400
Farm.

01:34:03.480 --> 01:34:07.200
That he was there earlier
in the day at Yew Tree Farm,

01:34:07.280 --> 01:34:11.120
or we have to start saying that
the reason he won't admit to

01:34:11.200 --> 01:34:14.760
that is because there is
something even greater that he's

01:34:14.840 --> 01:34:19.560
going to have to admit to which
was that he probably was the

01:34:19.640 --> 01:34:21.000
killer of Carl Bridgewater.

01:34:21.080 --> 01:34:25.880
- James D'Arcy: Professor David
Wilson has spent the last six

01:34:25.960 --> 01:34:28.920
months looking into the
murder of newspaper boy Carl

01:34:29.000 --> 01:34:30.400
Bridgewater.

01:34:30.440 --> 01:34:34.440
This is the fourth and final
meeting between the two men.

01:34:34.520 --> 01:34:36.880
- Bert Spencer: You'll be
tormenting somebody else

01:34:36.920 --> 01:34:37.520
tomorrow.

01:34:37.600 --> 01:34:39.480
- David Wilson: Come in.

01:34:39.560 --> 01:34:43.840
- Bert Spencer: Are all
your people as blunt as me?

01:34:43.920 --> 01:34:47.680
- James D'Arcy: Professor Wilson
is about to present Bert with

01:34:47.720 --> 01:34:51.720
his final findings and offer
his own evaluation of Bert's

01:34:51.760 --> 01:34:53.560
psychological state.

01:34:53.640 --> 01:34:54.360
- David Wilson: Bring
your Coke if you want.

01:34:54.400 --> 01:34:55.120
- Bert Spencer: I will do.

01:34:55.160 --> 01:34:56.520
Thank you, very much.

01:34:56.600 --> 01:35:00.640
Come on, then.

01:35:00.720 --> 01:35:01.040
- David Wilson: Are you alright?

01:35:01.120 --> 01:35:01.640
Comfortable?

01:35:01.720 --> 01:35:03.640
- Bert Spencer: Yes.

01:35:03.720 --> 01:35:05.840
- David Wilson: I think
the phrase that you used,

01:35:05.920 --> 01:35:09.520
that I always remember was that
you said to me that you wouldn't

01:35:09.600 --> 01:35:11.080
run, and you wouldn't hide.

01:35:11.160 --> 01:35:12.280
- Bert Spencer: I never do.

01:35:12.320 --> 01:35:15.160
- David Wilson: Your alibi
is that you were at Colbert

01:35:15.240 --> 01:35:21.640
Hospital all day on the day that
Carl Bridgewater was murdered.

01:35:21.720 --> 01:35:24.960
There was another person at
Colbert Hospital that day,

01:35:25.040 --> 01:35:26.560
who of course was, Barbara.

01:35:26.640 --> 01:35:29.160
- Bert Spencer: What
about the others?

01:35:29.200 --> 01:35:30.880
- David Wilson: Barbara in
particular--

01:35:30.960 --> 01:35:33.120
- Bert Spencer: She made a written statement, I believe.

01:35:33.200 --> 01:35:34.760
- David Wilson: She made
several written statements.

01:35:34.800 --> 01:35:35.480
- Bert Spencer: Okay.

01:35:35.560 --> 01:35:36.440
I didn't know that.

01:35:36.520 --> 01:35:40.920
Others who worked in the same
floor space, made verbal--"Yes.

01:35:41.000 --> 01:35:43.560
Okay, he was here," but
they didn't write it down,

01:35:43.640 --> 01:35:45.160
they didn't have to.

01:35:45.240 --> 01:35:49.160
The cops at the time didn't do
everything right--what I think

01:35:49.240 --> 01:35:49.920
is right.

01:35:49.960 --> 01:35:52.520
They didn't ask, "Will
you make a statement,

01:35:52.560 --> 01:35:54.320
will you make a statement?"

01:35:54.360 --> 01:35:57.560
They didn't feel the need,
someone has already done it.

01:35:57.640 --> 01:36:01.520
- David Wilson: And Barbara
made those statements.

01:36:01.560 --> 01:36:02.320
- Bert Spencer: Yes, yeah.

01:36:02.360 --> 01:36:04.000
- David Wilson: And as you
know, I've tracked down those

01:36:04.080 --> 01:36:07.400
statements, and you were
there when I asked her,

01:36:07.440 --> 01:36:14.080
could she say that she was there
all afternoon and was able to

01:36:14.160 --> 01:36:18.200
verify that you were
there all afternoon.

01:36:18.280 --> 01:36:19.520
And she can't.

01:36:19.600 --> 01:36:24.080
She can't guarantee what times
you may have left Colbert

01:36:24.120 --> 01:36:25.760
Hospital all afternoon.

01:36:25.840 --> 01:36:28.920
Means that you don't
have cast iron alibi.

01:36:28.960 --> 01:36:30.160
- Bert Spencer: That's fair.

01:36:30.200 --> 01:36:33.360
- David Wilson: That alibi
is--well, it disappears, Bert.

01:36:33.440 --> 01:36:36.160
- Bert Spencer: I
accept what you say.

01:36:36.240 --> 01:36:38.880
I don't have a cast iron alibi.

01:36:41.240 --> 01:36:43.800
Do you have any evidence that
I killed Carl Bridgewater?

01:36:43.840 --> 01:36:46.200
If you do, Sir,
bring it forward.

01:36:46.240 --> 01:36:49.240
If you don't, shut your mouth.

01:36:49.320 --> 01:36:49.920
Okay?

01:36:50.000 --> 01:36:50.840
You've now annoyed me.

01:36:50.880 --> 01:36:53.480
- David Wilson: You get
annoyed by me fairly regularly;

01:36:53.560 --> 01:36:55.160
Bert and you threaten
me quite a lot.

01:36:55.240 --> 01:36:56.480
- Bert Spencer: That's
not threatening,

01:36:56.560 --> 01:36:57.280
it's me talking straight.

01:36:57.320 --> 01:36:59.640
- David Wilson: I
also asked you, Bert,

01:36:59.720 --> 01:37:02.800
in relation to the
Wilkes' connection.

01:37:02.880 --> 01:37:06.120
What was happening the night
that you took Hubert Wilkes'

01:37:06.160 --> 01:37:07.040
life?

01:37:07.120 --> 01:37:11.640
Your wife at the time made
statements to various places

01:37:11.720 --> 01:37:14.680
saying that Carl Bridgewater
was mentioned that night.

01:37:14.760 --> 01:37:17.080
- Bert Spencer: Well, if it was,
I wasn't in the room at that

01:37:17.160 --> 01:37:17.880
time.

01:37:17.960 --> 01:37:19.080
I didn't mention it.

01:37:19.160 --> 01:37:21.600
- David Wilson: She says it
was mentioned at the time.

01:37:21.640 --> 01:37:22.320
- Bert Spencer: I don't
care what she says,

01:37:22.400 --> 01:37:23.880
I'm not rubbishing
her statement.

01:37:23.960 --> 01:37:28.400
I'm saying to you, I wasn't in
the room when it was--if it was.

01:37:28.480 --> 01:37:31.320
You can't just say, "But she
says it was." That's what I'm

01:37:31.400 --> 01:37:33.080
saying to you,
that's my response.

01:37:33.120 --> 01:37:34.760
- David Wilson: Hubert
Wilkes was going to you,

01:37:34.840 --> 01:37:39.080
I wonder if that plays a part in
relation to what happened at-- -

01:37:39.160 --> 01:37:43.040
Bert Spencer: You've just taken
one statement off one person and

01:37:43.120 --> 01:37:45.080
that's your God, your law.

01:37:45.160 --> 01:37:51.480
My first wife fires venom at me.

01:37:51.560 --> 01:37:52.640
Okay?

01:37:52.720 --> 01:37:56.320
For four years, after
I was convicted,

01:37:56.400 --> 01:37:58.280
it was all lovey-dovey
love letters.

01:37:58.360 --> 01:38:00.680
She was visiting, while in
my scrubs until suddenly,

01:38:00.720 --> 01:38:05.280
she found another man, and
she came out with this crap.

01:38:05.320 --> 01:38:06.320
- David Wilson: Bert--
- Bert Spencer: It is rubbish.

01:38:06.400 --> 01:38:09.920
- David Wilson: Did you and
Hubert Wilkes ever discuss

01:38:10.000 --> 01:38:11.560
robberies?

01:38:11.600 --> 01:38:12.640
- Bert Spencer: We never did.

01:38:12.720 --> 01:38:13.520
- David Wilson: Right.

01:38:13.560 --> 01:38:14.400
Okay.

01:38:14.480 --> 01:38:18.400
Now, I'm going to play you
something, Bert, at the moment.

01:38:18.480 --> 01:38:24.640
I particularly wanted you to
hear what your daughter said in

01:38:24.680 --> 01:38:29.440
relation to Hubert Wilkes and
the discussion of robberies.

01:38:29.520 --> 01:38:33.240
- Bert Spencer's Daughter: "I
particularly remember he would

01:38:33.280 --> 01:38:36.080
stand at the fire with
his back to the fire,

01:38:36.160 --> 01:38:37.840
and I was lying in
the [INDISCERNIBLE].

01:38:37.920 --> 01:38:43.400
He would just constantly
talk about robberies.

01:38:43.480 --> 01:38:48.240
And how you could commit the
perfect crime by escaping

01:38:48.320 --> 01:38:51.400
through the woods, and I--"
- Bert Spencer: I'm sorry,

01:38:51.480 --> 01:38:53.080
I don't agree with that.

01:38:53.160 --> 01:38:57.600
Hubert Wilkes used to come to
my house and drink whiskey.

01:38:57.640 --> 01:38:59.440
Never discussed
robberies with me.

01:38:59.480 --> 01:39:00.800
- David Wilson: So,
your daughter is wrong.

01:39:00.840 --> 01:39:02.160
- Bert Spencer:
She's made a mistake.

01:39:02.240 --> 01:39:03.640
- David Wilson: Well, she
doesn't think that, Bert.

01:39:03.680 --> 01:39:06.440
- Bert Spencer: Okay, a lot
of people think I killed Carl

01:39:06.480 --> 01:39:07.400
Bridgewater.

01:39:07.480 --> 01:39:09.760
I know I didn't, but I
can't change their opinion.

01:39:09.840 --> 01:39:12.560
- David Wilson: This is really
where I'm coming to you.

01:39:12.640 --> 01:39:13.200
Would you mind if I
showed you something?

01:39:13.280 --> 01:39:14.800
- Bert Spencer: Show me.

01:39:14.880 --> 01:39:15.920
- David Wilson: These
are distressing.

01:39:16.000 --> 01:39:17.840
- Bert Spencer:
[INDISCERNIBLE] mind, show me.

01:39:17.920 --> 01:39:18.960
- David Wilson: These are
distressing photographs.

01:39:19.040 --> 01:39:20.320
- Bert Spencer: Show me.

01:39:20.360 --> 01:39:23.360
- David Wilson: These are the
crime scene photographs of Carl

01:39:23.440 --> 01:39:24.400
Bridgewater.

01:39:24.480 --> 01:39:25.920
- Bert Spencer: Alright.

01:39:26.000 --> 01:39:27.480
That's at the scene, I assume.

01:39:27.560 --> 01:39:28.000
- David Wilson: Yeah.

01:39:28.080 --> 01:39:29.200
- Bert Spencer: Yes, okay.

01:39:29.280 --> 01:39:34.040
- David Wilson: I've shown you
that because I think with the

01:39:34.120 --> 01:39:38.280
fact that you no
longer have an alibi,

01:39:38.320 --> 01:39:43.360
that something happened that day
that involved you at Yew Tree

01:39:43.440 --> 01:39:46.400
Farm and I want you
to listen again.

01:39:46.480 --> 01:39:49.240
- Bert Spencer Daughter:
"I've always felt that he saw

01:39:49.320 --> 01:39:54.560
something that day
that killed him inside.

01:39:54.640 --> 01:39:59.880
If a story ever came out
like, "I finished my shift,

01:39:59.960 --> 01:40:03.360
and I've got a little bit
of time and I thought,

01:40:03.400 --> 01:40:07.440
I'll go and do that errand," and
then suddenly hear a gunshot go

01:40:07.520 --> 01:40:15.840
off, and then, after it's
all dispersed--and you see

01:40:15.880 --> 01:40:17.200
something."

01:40:17.280 --> 01:40:17.960
- Bert Spencer: Tell, how come--

01:40:18.040 --> 01:40:19.800
- Bert Spencer's
Daughter: "On what you're

01:40:19.880 --> 01:40:21.160
witnessing and
what you're seeing,

01:40:21.240 --> 01:40:22.920
and you're just horrified.

01:40:23.000 --> 01:40:27.360
I have tried to discuss that
with him on an occasion,

01:40:27.400 --> 01:40:31.560
but he was very emotional."

01:40:31.600 --> 01:40:32.040
- David Wilson:
Was that what happened?

01:40:32.120 --> 01:40:32.600
- Bert Spencer: No.

01:40:32.680 --> 01:40:33.800
- David Wilson: Did you go
to Yew Tree Farm that day?

01:40:33.880 --> 01:40:36.920
Your daughter thinks that
you did go to Yew Tree Farm.

01:40:37.000 --> 01:40:38.240
- Bert Spencer: Yes, I
just heard what you said,

01:40:38.320 --> 01:40:40.800
she also thinks it
was after my shift.

01:40:40.840 --> 01:40:42.520
After 5:00 PM.

01:40:42.600 --> 01:40:43.520
Too late.

01:40:43.600 --> 01:40:45.120
Too late, mate.

01:40:45.200 --> 01:40:47.040
Even if I got there a
quarter past, 20 past.

01:40:47.080 --> 01:40:49.280
There's no gunshot,
that was an hour ago.

01:40:49.360 --> 01:40:50.920
- David Wilson: Did you
go after your shift?

01:40:50.960 --> 01:40:52.320
- Bert Spencer: No, I did not.

01:40:52.400 --> 01:40:54.400
- David Wilson: You've
forgotten the time, she said.

01:40:54.480 --> 01:40:55.920
- David Wilson: Did you
go after your shift?

01:40:55.960 --> 01:40:56.920
- Bert Spencer: No,
for Christ's sake.

01:40:56.960 --> 01:40:58.640
What for?

01:40:58.720 --> 01:40:59.120
What?

01:40:59.160 --> 01:41:01.760
I wasn't working
there that month.

01:41:01.800 --> 01:41:03.920
What for?

01:41:04.000 --> 01:41:06.800
- David Wilson: Well,
after our first interview,

01:41:06.840 --> 01:41:09.160
I did a P Scan on you.

01:41:09.200 --> 01:41:09.960
- Bert Spencer: What's that?

01:41:10.000 --> 01:41:12.040
- David Wilson: It
means, psychopathy scan.

01:41:12.120 --> 01:41:14.440
So, this isn't a
clinical diagnosis,

01:41:14.480 --> 01:41:19.120
this is a way that I can gain
some insight into the person

01:41:19.200 --> 01:41:24.720
that I'm talking to and the
total score for you was in the

01:41:24.800 --> 01:41:26.160
high range.

01:41:26.240 --> 01:41:29.320
A total score that falls in the
high range should be course for

01:41:29.400 --> 01:41:31.080
serious concern.

01:41:31.160 --> 01:41:34.880
It suggests that the person of
interest may have many or most

01:41:34.960 --> 01:41:37.520
of the features that
define psychopathy.

01:41:37.600 --> 01:41:42.520
Such a person is likely
to be egocentric, callous,

01:41:42.600 --> 01:41:46.760
cold-blooded, predatory,
impulsive, irresponsible,

01:41:46.840 --> 01:41:51.080
dominant, deceptive,
manipulative and lacking in

01:41:51.160 --> 01:41:56.480
empathy, guild or genuine
remorse for socially deviant and

01:41:56.520 --> 01:41:58.880
criminal acts.

01:41:58.920 --> 01:42:00.920
And that fits you to a T.

01:42:01.000 --> 01:42:02.920
- Bert Spencer: It also
fits you, you bugger,

01:42:02.960 --> 01:42:04.400
some of the things you've said.

01:42:04.480 --> 01:42:05.160
To a T.

01:42:05.240 --> 01:42:07.160
- David Wilson:
This is you, Bert.

01:42:07.200 --> 01:42:09.120
- Bert Spencer: So, you've
just condemned me, haven't you?

01:42:09.160 --> 01:42:12.120
- David Wilson: You are somebody
that I regard-- - Bert Spencer:

01:42:12.200 --> 01:42:13.200
As a psychopath.

01:42:13.280 --> 01:42:17.040
- David Wilson: As a liar, as
somebody who has bent the truth.

01:42:17.080 --> 01:42:19.880
As somebody who is
manipulative, as coning.

01:42:19.960 --> 01:42:23.120
You are not this kindly
old grandfather figure,

01:42:23.200 --> 01:42:27.760
that is your shtick, Bert, and I
see through it and all I can say

01:42:27.840 --> 01:42:33.040
to you is, in the absence
of you having any alibi,

01:42:33.120 --> 01:42:37.040
because you were there when
Barbara says she cannot stand up

01:42:37.120 --> 01:42:40.320
where you were on the afternoon
that Carl Bridgewater was

01:42:40.360 --> 01:42:40.960
murdered.

01:42:41.000 --> 01:42:41.840
- Bert Spencer: I have
an answer for you.

01:42:41.920 --> 01:42:44.920
- David Wilson: I am going to
be taking this material to the

01:42:45.000 --> 01:42:45.760
police.

01:42:45.800 --> 01:42:46.240
- Bert Spencer: Yeah.

01:42:46.320 --> 01:42:49.440
- David Wilson: Because I think
this case needs to be reopened.

01:42:49.520 --> 01:42:51.560
- Bert Spencer: Right,
I'm not going to say,

01:42:51.640 --> 01:42:54.160
"Let me get a word in," I am
going to get a word in, David.

01:42:54.240 --> 01:42:57.640
I think you need to
see a psychiatrist.

01:42:57.680 --> 01:42:59.280
You are wrong.

01:42:59.360 --> 01:43:04.040
So very wrong, your
egoistic, all Mr. Important,

01:43:04.120 --> 01:43:07.120
what the hell does it
matter what you think?

01:43:07.200 --> 01:43:11.080
The evidence shows how the hell
can I be there when I wasn't

01:43:11.160 --> 01:43:12.560
there?

01:43:12.640 --> 01:43:14.440
You'd like to prove that?

01:43:14.520 --> 01:43:17.640
Go ahead, go what you want, but
I think you need psychiatric

01:43:17.720 --> 01:43:18.360
treatment, son.

01:43:18.400 --> 01:43:20.680
There's something wrong
with the way that you think.

01:43:20.760 --> 01:43:21.520
That is my belief.

01:43:21.600 --> 01:43:23.800
- David Wilson: I think now,
it's really a question for other

01:43:23.840 --> 01:43:26.160
people to make a decision
on these kinds of things.

01:43:26.240 --> 01:43:28.440
- Bert Spencer: Let them do it.

01:43:28.480 --> 01:43:29.960
- David Wilson: All I can
do is tell you what I felt,

01:43:30.040 --> 01:43:32.040
and I will raise this elsewhere.

01:43:32.080 --> 01:43:33.040
- Bert Spencer: Okay, do that.

01:43:33.080 --> 01:43:36.040
- David Wilson: Thank you.

01:43:40.000 --> 01:43:42.320
- Bert Spencer: Big
silence in the room,

01:43:42.400 --> 01:43:44.360
he's a fucking nut case.

01:43:44.440 --> 01:43:48.120
I didn't know that,
I didn't know that.

01:43:48.160 --> 01:43:51.560
- David Wilson: He knows that if
he starts admitting that he was

01:43:51.600 --> 01:43:56.120
at Yew Tree Farm, then actually,
then the police have got to be

01:43:56.200 --> 01:43:57.320
re-interviewing him.

01:43:57.400 --> 01:43:59.600
- Bert Spencer: It's all a
load of absolute nonsense,

01:43:59.640 --> 01:44:01.400
it really is.

01:44:01.440 --> 01:44:03.880
- Christine: I could not
believe what I was hearing.

01:44:03.960 --> 01:44:06.520
- Bert Spencer: He can't work
it out, so instead of filling,

01:44:06.600 --> 01:44:08.320
he wasn't me as a scapegoat.

01:44:08.400 --> 01:44:12.240
- Christine: I'll
certainly stand by my man.

01:44:12.320 --> 01:44:13.720
Every inch of the way.

01:44:13.800 --> 01:44:16.520
- Bert Spencer: This
psychopath needs a cuddle.

01:44:16.560 --> 01:44:22.040
- David Wilson: I personally
still think with the fact that

01:44:22.120 --> 01:44:25.360
Barbara can no longer
stand up his alibi,

01:44:25.400 --> 01:44:28.280
this case should be reopened.

01:44:28.360 --> 01:44:29.040
- Bert Spencer: Alright.

01:44:31.960 --> 01:44:56.760
(music playing)

01:44:56.800 --> 01:44:59.560
- David Wilson: As ever in
the world of Bert Spencer,

01:44:59.600 --> 01:45:02.800
just when you think you've
got some kind of conclusion,

01:45:02.880 --> 01:45:06.840
other possibilities immediately
offer themselves up.

01:45:06.920 --> 01:45:08.840
Somebody has made
contact with us,

01:45:08.880 --> 01:45:11.800
that I've been
wanting to speak to,

01:45:11.880 --> 01:45:14.680
throughout this entire process.

01:45:14.760 --> 01:45:20.920
Bert's first wife, Janet has
never spoken before on camera

01:45:21.000 --> 01:45:26.120
and has maintained a
silence for some 38 years.

01:45:26.160 --> 01:45:28.320
I don't know what
she's going to say,

01:45:28.360 --> 01:45:36.880
but I know that whatever she
says is going to be absolutely

01:45:36.960 --> 01:45:37.640
fascinating.

01:45:37.720 --> 01:45:40.880
Janet, how long were you
married to Bert Spencer for?

01:45:40.960 --> 01:45:44.000
- Janet: About 16-17 years.

01:45:44.080 --> 01:45:45.960
- David Wilson: Was he
popular with the girls?

01:45:46.040 --> 01:45:47.040
- Janet: Yes, yes.

01:45:47.080 --> 01:45:49.280
With everyone, he
was very well-loved.

01:45:49.320 --> 01:45:54.640
- David Wilson: When you got to
really know him for all those

01:45:54.720 --> 01:45:56.800
years, was there another
side to him, as well?

01:45:56.880 --> 01:45:57.480
- Janet: Oh, yes.

01:45:57.560 --> 01:45:58.680
Yes.

01:45:58.760 --> 01:46:02.960
- David Wilson: Describe
that other side for me.

01:46:03.040 --> 01:46:05.840
- Janet: Dark.

01:46:05.920 --> 01:46:09.360
He could be very
volatile at times,

01:46:09.400 --> 01:46:11.240
if things upset
him or--you know,

01:46:11.320 --> 01:46:17.480
he had a bit too much to drink,
he could change drastically.

01:46:17.560 --> 01:46:19.480
- David Wilson: Was
he ever nasty to you?

01:46:19.520 --> 01:46:20.360
- Janet: Oh, yes.

01:46:20.440 --> 01:46:21.160
Yeah.

01:46:21.240 --> 01:46:24.000
- David Wilson: In what way?

01:46:24.080 --> 01:46:27.040
- Janet: Oh, well,
many--he's hit me.

01:46:27.120 --> 01:46:32.960
He hit me on a few occasions,
but it was just very frightening

01:46:33.040 --> 01:46:36.560
and menacing when
he lost his temper.

01:46:36.640 --> 01:46:38.680
He could be very intimidating.

01:46:38.760 --> 01:46:40.040
- David Wilson: Did
he intimidate you?

01:46:40.120 --> 01:46:41.360
- Janet: Yes, yeah.

01:46:41.400 --> 01:46:43.960
- David Wilson: It sounds as if
your marriage was like walking

01:46:44.040 --> 01:46:45.440
on eggshells.

01:46:45.480 --> 01:46:47.440
- Janet: A lot of
the time, yeah.

01:46:47.520 --> 01:46:50.440
(music playing)

01:46:50.480 --> 01:46:52.280
- James D'Arcy: Janet was
present the night that farmer,

01:46:52.320 --> 01:46:54.320
Hubert Wilkes, was killed.

01:46:54.400 --> 01:46:57.480
The murder has been described as
a carbon copy of the killing of

01:46:57.520 --> 01:47:01.840
Carl Bridgewater, but what
really happened that night and

01:47:01.920 --> 01:47:04.720
did it have anything to do
with the murder of Carl?

01:47:04.760 --> 01:47:10.360
- David Wilson: Can I take you
back to the night that Hubert

01:47:10.400 --> 01:47:13.240
Wilkes was murdered?

01:47:13.320 --> 01:47:15.880
Can you describe to
me what happened?

01:47:15.960 --> 01:47:22.840
- Janet: When out to the farm,
I went into the house with Bert,

01:47:22.880 --> 01:47:29.720
went into the dining room where
I used to carry out some--where

01:47:29.800 --> 01:47:33.160
the payroll [INDISCERNIBLE], and
then eventually when I finished

01:47:33.240 --> 01:47:35.720
the work that I had to do.

01:47:35.760 --> 01:47:42.360
I joined them in the lounge and
my first reaction when I saw

01:47:42.400 --> 01:47:47.200
Bert was that he seemed to have
quite a lot to drink in the

01:47:47.240 --> 01:47:48.600
actual time.

01:47:48.640 --> 01:47:53.360
Jane had mentioned a book
that she had been reading,

01:47:53.440 --> 01:47:59.200
and it sounded interesting, so
she went upstairs to fetch the

01:47:59.280 --> 01:48:00.920
book for me.

01:48:01.000 --> 01:48:06.840
And then immediately,
she'd done that,

01:48:06.920 --> 01:48:11.040
Bert said that he was
going to go to the toilet.

01:48:11.120 --> 01:48:15.360
So, off he toddles, and I'm
sitting with Mr. Wilkes in the

01:48:15.400 --> 01:48:19.720
sitting, and he seemed to
have been gone for some time,

01:48:19.800 --> 01:48:24.800
and I was getting
concerned, if he was okay.

01:48:24.840 --> 01:48:31.040
And after that, Bert appeared,
I said, "Oh, there you are,

01:48:31.080 --> 01:48:42.280
" and then he sort of slopped
in and then turned around fully,

01:48:42.320 --> 01:48:45.600
and I could see that he
could get a gun in his hand,

01:48:45.680 --> 01:48:49.160
and then he just
shot Mr. Wilkes.

01:48:49.200 --> 01:48:52.840
Severed the head, while he
was sitting in the sitting.

01:48:52.920 --> 01:48:54.000
- David Wilson: What
did he do to you?

01:48:54.080 --> 01:49:01.600
- Janet: Well, immediately after
that had happened, I thought,

01:49:01.640 --> 01:49:08.680
"I'm next," and in the split
second that he--he hadn't turned

01:49:08.760 --> 01:49:10.200
the gun on me.

01:49:10.280 --> 01:49:15.800
I ran out of there and grabbed
a gun with two hands and shouted

01:49:15.880 --> 01:49:19.040
at him, "Do you realize
what you've done?"

01:49:19.120 --> 01:49:24.920
But at that point, Jane came
into the far door again,

01:49:25.000 --> 01:49:28.200
took one look, screamed and ran.

01:49:29.160 --> 01:49:31.320
So, he took off after Jane.

01:49:31.400 --> 01:49:38.960
So, I ran into the kitchen,
tried to unlock the door and I

01:49:39.040 --> 01:49:41.920
couldn't unlock the door.

01:49:42.000 --> 01:49:48.560
And it was in darkness and I ran
and hid under the kitchen table,

01:49:48.640 --> 01:49:52.760
and I [INDISCERNIBLE], I could
hear the gun clatter on the

01:49:52.800 --> 01:49:56.720
floor, and I thought, "He is
going to hear that."

01:49:56.760 --> 01:50:03.240
He came running in again, and he
ran out into the yard.

01:50:03.320 --> 01:50:05.640
- Bert Spencer: "Janet!"

01:50:05.680 --> 01:50:09.240
- Janet: I went through the
bottom door into the hallway,

01:50:09.280 --> 01:50:15.120
picked up the phone, and he
appeared from the other door

01:50:15.200 --> 01:50:16.880
down the hall with another gun.

01:50:16.920 --> 01:50:24.240
A complete gun, not a cut-off
one and he had proceeded to hit

01:50:24.320 --> 01:50:31.840
me in the head with it, time
after time, until I went down,

01:50:31.920 --> 01:50:40.200
and I was thinking, "Now, this
is it." And then Jane came in

01:50:40.240 --> 01:50:42.800
again, through the door, in
the hallway and screamed,

01:50:42.840 --> 01:50:45.040
and he ran after her again.

01:50:45.080 --> 01:50:48.600
So, [INDISCERNIBLE],
I've never been out,

01:50:48.680 --> 01:50:57.760
but [INDISCERNIBLE] saved me,
and--after that it was just a

01:50:57.840 --> 01:51:02.880
question of trying to escape
the farm without him finding me.

01:51:02.960 --> 01:51:05.200
- David Wilson: And you've
got absolutely no doubt he was

01:51:05.240 --> 01:51:07.600
trying to kill you.

01:51:07.680 --> 01:51:12.240
- Janet: Well...he
didn't shoot me.

01:51:12.280 --> 01:51:17.720
I can't understand why
he didn't--a few more,

01:51:17.800 --> 01:51:19.200
he probably would've killed me.

01:51:19.280 --> 01:51:22.440
A few more hits across the head.

01:51:22.480 --> 01:51:26.200
- David Wilson: On the night
in question in December,

01:51:26.280 --> 01:51:31.360
had there been discussion about
the Bridgewater case and the

01:51:31.400 --> 01:51:35.480
appeal being lodged by
the Bridgewater Four?

01:51:35.560 --> 01:51:39.880
- Janet: Well, because I was
in the--office for most of the

01:51:39.960 --> 01:51:42.840
time, I can't--I can't say.

01:51:42.920 --> 01:51:46.840
I mean, it has been
mentioned since.

01:51:46.880 --> 01:51:50.960
I'm not sure--we did see
something on the television once

01:51:51.040 --> 01:51:54.600
together, Bert and I, that they
were appealing against their

01:51:54.680 --> 01:51:55.680
conviction.

01:51:55.720 --> 01:52:01.840
I'm not sure if it was
that night, but he did say,

01:52:01.920 --> 01:52:05.760
"If they get off, they'll be
after me." - David Wilson: Did

01:52:05.840 --> 01:52:10.680
he ever explain to you
why he shot Hubert Wilkes?

01:52:10.760 --> 01:52:11.320
- Janet: No, never.

01:52:11.400 --> 01:52:16.120
Never--he said he
had a black-out.

01:52:16.200 --> 01:52:20.040
He never gave any
logical explanation.

01:52:20.120 --> 01:52:22.560
No.

01:52:22.640 --> 01:52:26.720
- David Wilson: Bert claims that
Mr. Wilkes was going to give you

01:52:26.800 --> 01:52:28.480
a special cocktail.

01:52:28.520 --> 01:52:31.640
So as to be able to
have sex with you.

01:52:31.720 --> 01:52:33.840
Is there any truth in that?

01:52:33.920 --> 01:52:36.160
Was that ever mentioned to you?

01:52:36.240 --> 01:52:37.320
- Janet: No.

01:52:37.400 --> 01:52:40.320
I know he's given that as a
reason because he's pretty down

01:52:40.360 --> 01:52:45.440
and writing to me, in the
following years, but no.

01:52:45.520 --> 01:52:47.000
I didn't know anything.

01:52:47.080 --> 01:52:51.240
If he had arranged something, I
didn't know anything about it.

01:52:51.280 --> 01:52:53.080
I don't believe it anyway.

01:52:53.160 --> 01:52:55.200
I just don't believe it.

01:52:55.240 --> 01:53:00.320
- David Wilson: Did you discuss
the Bridgewater case with Bert?

01:53:00.400 --> 01:53:03.000
I mean, here's the
bit that confuses me.

01:53:03.080 --> 01:53:06.120
I went back to Ascot Gardens
where you used to live and the

01:53:06.160 --> 01:53:09.240
Bridgewater's lived
a few doors up.

01:53:09.320 --> 01:53:10.560
- Janet: That's right.

01:53:10.640 --> 01:53:11.640
- David Wilson: Did
you socialize with the

01:53:11.720 --> 01:53:13.280
Bridgewater's?

01:53:13.360 --> 01:53:16.600
- Janet: We didn't actually
socialize with them,

01:53:16.680 --> 01:53:20.040
but I know on one occasion
when we had a partner,

01:53:20.120 --> 01:53:21.400
they came and had a drink.

01:53:21.480 --> 01:53:25.680
You know, at the house,
except for loads of people,

01:53:25.760 --> 01:53:28.640
there were quite a
few, actually, but yes,

01:53:28.720 --> 01:53:29.440
they did come out.

01:53:29.520 --> 01:53:32.280
So, we knew them because
they were our neighbors,

01:53:32.360 --> 01:53:33.040
we knew the son.

01:53:33.080 --> 01:53:35.160
- David Wilson: Carl.

01:53:35.240 --> 01:53:38.680
- Janet: Yeah, so, I can't
understand why he says he

01:53:38.720 --> 01:53:40.720
doesn't know them because I did.

01:53:40.760 --> 01:53:44.560
- David Wilson: Janet, can
you describe what you remember

01:53:44.640 --> 01:53:51.280
happening on the day that Carl
was murdered when you went home?

01:53:51.360 --> 01:53:56.200
- Janet: I came into the house
and I saw Bert standing there

01:53:56.280 --> 01:54:04.240
and--with his hands in his
pockets, bloody language,

01:54:04.320 --> 01:54:10.080
telling me that he
wasn't very happy.

01:54:10.160 --> 01:54:14.520
And I also noticed a jumper
had been pegged on the line,

01:54:14.560 --> 01:54:19.000
which--which a man had put up
because women don't peg jumpers

01:54:19.080 --> 01:54:19.880
on like that.

01:54:19.960 --> 01:54:22.040
- David Wilson: Was that
one of Bert's work jumpers?

01:54:22.120 --> 01:54:23.400
What color was it?

01:54:23.480 --> 01:54:25.800
- Janet: No, it was
a casual jumper.

01:54:25.880 --> 01:54:26.440
Green.

01:54:26.520 --> 01:54:29.440
Green--jumper.

01:54:29.520 --> 01:54:32.400
- David Wilson: Did you
ever see that jumper again?

01:54:32.440 --> 01:54:33.760
- Janet: No, never.

01:54:33.840 --> 01:54:37.440
- David Wilson: But was it clear
to you that Bert had washed the

01:54:37.520 --> 01:54:38.000
jumper?

01:54:38.080 --> 01:54:38.960
- Janet: Oh yeah.

01:54:39.040 --> 01:54:40.400
- David Wilson: It
just disappeared.

01:54:40.480 --> 01:54:43.880
- Janet: Just disappeared.

01:54:43.920 --> 01:54:46.600
He went to bed, which he did.

01:54:46.680 --> 01:54:50.920
He went to bed early and I
then watched the news on the

01:54:51.000 --> 01:54:52.400
television.

01:54:52.480 --> 01:55:01.600
And as I watched it
and--everything about it,

01:55:01.680 --> 01:55:09.160
you know, the fact who he
was, where he lived, the boy,

01:55:09.240 --> 01:55:11.240
and what had happened to him.

01:55:11.280 --> 01:55:13.960
[INDISCERNIBLE],
couldn't believe it.

01:55:14.040 --> 01:55:17.400
Washed, you know,
washed all over.

01:55:17.480 --> 01:55:28.080
I went upstairs and
shook his shoulder,

01:55:28.160 --> 01:55:32.200
he wasn't asleep and
said to him, "Please God,

01:55:32.280 --> 01:55:38.040
please tell me you didn't have
anything to do with that." And

01:55:38.080 --> 01:55:45.560
he said, "No, I didn't, but
don't you think they'd be after

01:55:45.640 --> 01:55:53.480
me, if it was my patch," or
words to that effect."

01:55:53.520 --> 01:55:54.720
That's what he said.

01:55:54.760 --> 01:55:58.360
- David Wilson: And that
must have been quite odd.

01:55:58.440 --> 01:56:01.120
How would he know about it?

01:56:01.160 --> 01:56:03.480
- Janet: Well, I've
wondered since, actually,

01:56:03.560 --> 01:56:07.000
but I can't assure that nothing
was on the news earlier,

01:56:07.080 --> 01:56:16.680
but I can't say how--how
he could've--I don't know.

01:56:16.760 --> 01:56:19.000
- David Wilson: But he wasn't
watching the news with you.

01:56:19.080 --> 01:56:20.080
- Janet: No, no.

01:56:20.160 --> 01:56:21.200
He'd gone to bed.

01:56:21.280 --> 01:56:23.440
- David Wilson: So,
you go upstairs.

01:56:23.480 --> 01:56:27.960
He's still awake, and you
ask him, "Please, God,

01:56:28.040 --> 01:56:31.360
tell me you had nothing to
do with that." And he knew

01:56:31.440 --> 01:56:33.720
immediately what you
were talking about.

01:56:33.760 --> 01:56:35.160
- Janet: Oh yeah, he did.

01:56:35.240 --> 01:56:38.400
- David Wilson:
Was Bert worried?

01:56:38.440 --> 01:56:41.760
- Janet: He was--he was
concerned, and I said,

01:56:41.800 --> 01:56:43.160
"I don't know why
you're worried,

01:56:43.200 --> 01:56:46.400
because you were at work." -
David Wilson: And what did he

01:56:46.440 --> 01:56:48.640
say to that?

01:56:48.720 --> 01:56:55.440
- Janet: He says, "I wasn't
there all day." - David Wilson:

01:56:55.480 --> 01:56:57.240
He said he wasn't
at work all day.

01:56:57.280 --> 01:57:00.760
- Janet: He said, "I wasn't
there all the time because--" I

01:57:00.840 --> 01:57:02.080
said, "Well?"

01:57:02.120 --> 01:57:08.120
He says, "Because I didn't
feel well." So, he says,

01:57:08.160 --> 01:57:10.560
"I was in the toilet
for a long time.

01:57:10.600 --> 01:57:13.200
A stomach problem.

01:57:13.280 --> 01:57:17.000
'' I said, "Surely,
someone must have seen you.

01:57:17.040 --> 01:57:20.520
Somebody must have heard you
or said you were going to the

01:57:20.600 --> 01:57:26.560
toilet to sit now."
And he says, "Well,

01:57:26.640 --> 01:57:31.840
the only one that could probably
say something is Barbara up the

01:57:31.920 --> 01:57:35.320
road." She lived a few
doors away, Barbara.

01:57:35.360 --> 01:57:36.920
So, he went and fetched her.

01:57:37.000 --> 01:57:38.640
- David Wilson: He
went and fetched her.

01:57:38.720 --> 01:57:41.040
- Janet: To come
down to the house.

01:57:41.120 --> 01:57:42.840
She said, "Yes, of
course," you know,

01:57:42.920 --> 01:57:46.720
"I can give a statement
to the police.

01:57:46.800 --> 01:57:52.360
Don't worry." - David Wilson:
Did you believe that statement?

01:57:52.440 --> 01:57:56.280
- Janet: Well, knowing what he
had said to me, I just thought,

01:57:56.360 --> 01:57:58.440
she was probably
helping him out,

01:57:58.480 --> 01:58:03.040
but it wasn't really that
important because he wasn't the

01:58:03.080 --> 01:58:07.560
culprit, or I was
hoping he wasn't.

01:58:07.640 --> 01:58:10.480
- David Wilson: Bert
clearly collected antiques,

01:58:10.520 --> 01:58:13.360
bought and sold antiques.

01:58:13.440 --> 01:58:20.360
Were you ever worried that some
of those antiques were stolen?

01:58:20.400 --> 01:58:24.840
- Janet: No, I never
suspected that at all.

01:58:24.920 --> 01:58:28.240
We did a lot of
wheeling and dealing,

01:58:28.280 --> 01:58:39.240
but a friend told me that a bag
that contained antiques had been

01:58:39.320 --> 01:58:45.160
found in their shed because
someone had seen Bert coming out

01:58:45.240 --> 01:58:48.040
of their driveway.

01:58:48.120 --> 01:58:52.560
And so, the husband went down
to have a look in the shed and

01:58:52.600 --> 01:58:58.880
found this bag of antiques,
brassware and copper.

01:58:59.200 --> 01:59:02.040
So, following on what
my friend had told me,

01:59:02.080 --> 01:59:06.000
when I went to visit
Bert in prison,

01:59:06.080 --> 01:59:09.880
I did question him
about this bag.

01:59:12.240 --> 01:59:17.680
And his words to me were, "You
must never ever tell anybody

01:59:17.720 --> 01:59:22.960
about that.
Ever."

01:59:23.000 --> 01:59:25.920
So, I said, "Well,
okay, but you know,

01:59:26.000 --> 01:59:27.200
what happened to them?"

01:59:27.280 --> 01:59:31.560
He said that he buried them
somewhere around the Crestwood

01:59:31.640 --> 01:59:34.840
area which is off the A449.

01:59:34.880 --> 01:59:38.160
- David Wilson: So, the
implication was that he had

01:59:38.240 --> 01:59:42.840
stolen those antiques, tried to
hide them in the shed of one of

01:59:42.880 --> 01:59:47.480
his friends and then when he
was caught having done so,

01:59:47.520 --> 01:59:51.720
challenged by your friend, he
then buried the antique that he

01:59:51.760 --> 01:59:53.720
had stolen.

01:59:53.760 --> 01:59:57.800
Where do those antiques
that were buried came from?

01:59:57.880 --> 02:00:00.720
Do you think they could have
come from Yew Tree Farm?

02:00:00.800 --> 02:00:07.280
- Janet: Well, that's got to be
the thinking of all of us as the

02:00:07.360 --> 02:00:10.720
friend asked him to move them,
and it was close to the Carl

02:00:10.800 --> 02:00:14.680
Bridgewater time.

02:00:14.760 --> 02:00:17.880
I can only assume that he's got
something that he shouldn't have

02:00:17.920 --> 02:00:20.040
had.

02:00:20.120 --> 02:00:23.040
- David Wilson: Janet,
in September 1978,

02:00:23.120 --> 02:00:27.360
did Bert own a shotgun?

02:00:27.400 --> 02:00:29.520
- Janet: Yes, he did.

02:00:29.560 --> 02:00:33.800
- David Wilson: What
happened to that shotgun?

02:00:33.840 --> 02:00:40.240
- Janet: I remember him saying,
probably the day after Carl

02:00:40.320 --> 02:00:47.680
Bridgewater--that Carl
Bridgewater murder, he ought to,

02:00:47.720 --> 02:00:48.720
you know, get rid of it.

02:00:48.800 --> 02:00:50.480
So, take it somewhere.

02:00:50.520 --> 02:00:53.920
And I don't know
what happened to it,

02:00:53.960 --> 02:00:58.400
but there was a gun in
the house at the time.

02:00:58.480 --> 02:01:00.840
And he did have a
shotgun license for it.

02:01:00.920 --> 02:01:04.600
- David Wilson: And he
disposed of the shotgun,

02:01:04.680 --> 02:01:09.480
the day after Carl Bridgewater--
- Janet: Got shot, yeah.

02:01:09.560 --> 02:01:14.080
- David Wilson: When you put
all the various pieces of

02:01:14.160 --> 02:01:20.120
information that you're in
possession of together now,

02:01:20.200 --> 02:01:25.640
what does it lead
you to conclude?

02:01:25.720 --> 02:01:28.400
- Janet: It's not
concrete evidence,

02:01:28.480 --> 02:01:40.000
but I really believe that
if he didn't actually shoot,

02:01:40.040 --> 02:01:46.800
he knows more about
that than he's let on.

02:01:46.840 --> 02:01:49.760
- David Wilson: Would it
surprise you if the truth was,

02:01:49.840 --> 02:01:53.120
Bert Spencer, your ex-husband
killed Carl Bridgewater?

02:01:53.200 --> 02:01:54.080
Would that surprise you?

02:01:54.160 --> 02:01:55.400
- Janet: No, it wouldn't.

02:01:55.480 --> 02:01:58.280
- David Wilson: In fact, do you
suspect that's what happened?

02:02:00.760 --> 02:02:07.400
- Janet: Well, you know, it's
hard to say, but deep down, yes.

02:02:07.480 --> 02:02:09.480
I probably--I do really, now.

02:02:09.560 --> 02:02:21.320
(music playing)

02:02:21.400 --> 02:02:23.160
- James D'Arcy: Following
Janet's damning claims,

02:02:23.240 --> 02:02:25.640
Bert is given a right
to reply to them.

02:02:30.800 --> 02:02:31.880
- Producer: On the
day of Carl's murder,

02:02:31.920 --> 02:02:35.680
Janet says she returned home to
find a green jumper hanging on

02:02:35.720 --> 02:02:37.320
the clothesline.

02:02:37.360 --> 02:02:40.600
She believes Bert hung it
there, she says he was behaving

02:02:40.680 --> 02:02:41.880
strangely.

02:02:41.960 --> 02:02:46.040
She says she never
saw that jersey again.

02:02:46.120 --> 02:02:47.800
- Bert Spencer: Neither did I.

02:02:47.840 --> 02:02:51.840
I have no idea what that means,
if you'd like to expand upon

02:02:51.920 --> 02:02:52.440
that.

02:02:55.360 --> 02:03:00.440
I was in uniform, don't
forget--if I was there.

02:03:00.480 --> 02:03:02.400
Green jumper?

02:03:02.480 --> 02:03:04.960
Sorry.

02:03:05.040 --> 02:03:07.480
- Producer: After returning
from a family celebration,

02:03:07.520 --> 02:03:12.160
Janet saw the news report
about Carl's murder on the TV,

02:03:12.240 --> 02:03:14.200
she rushed to tell
Bert who was in bed,

02:03:14.280 --> 02:03:18.320
saying Carl had been killed at
Yew Tree Farm, and she said,

02:03:18.400 --> 02:03:22.000
"Please God, please tell me
you had nothing to do with it,

02:03:22.080 --> 02:03:24.560
" to which Bert
replied, "No, I didn't,

02:03:24.600 --> 02:03:26.560
but don't you think
they'll be after me?"

02:03:26.640 --> 02:03:28.520
- Bert Spencer: Oh,
that's nonsense.

02:03:28.600 --> 02:03:32.680
I did not say that I
don't agree with that.

02:03:32.720 --> 02:03:36.960
- Producer: Janet said Bert
added the following explanation.

02:03:37.040 --> 02:03:41.760
"It happened on my patch." She
never understood why he made

02:03:41.840 --> 02:03:45.560
that remark but realizes
now how odd it is.

02:03:45.640 --> 02:03:48.760
- Bert Spencer: I would never
have made that remark and don't

02:03:48.800 --> 02:03:50.400
accept it.

02:03:50.480 --> 02:03:55.280
- Producer: According to Janet,
Bert initially told her that he

02:03:55.360 --> 02:03:58.880
didn't have a proper alibi at
work because he had been in the

02:03:58.960 --> 02:04:02.640
toilet most of the afternoon,
due to a bad tummy.

02:04:02.720 --> 02:04:06.480
She said, later, he physically
fetched Barbara from up the

02:04:06.520 --> 02:04:09.200
road, in order to
give him an alibi.

02:04:09.280 --> 02:04:13.040
- Bert Spencer: What
absolute nonsense.

02:04:13.120 --> 02:04:17.640
Read the police
statements from Barbara.

02:04:17.720 --> 02:04:21.600
- Producer: Janet said that
Bert had a shotgun and a shotgun

02:04:21.680 --> 02:04:25.800
license but disposed of the
shotgun the day after Carl

02:04:25.880 --> 02:04:27.400
Bridgewater was killed.

02:04:27.440 --> 02:04:30.600
- Bert Spencer: No, I disposed
of the shotgun--I used to buy

02:04:30.640 --> 02:04:31.840
and sell shotguns.

02:04:31.880 --> 02:04:36.240
I had a shotgun license, I
disposed of it about two months

02:04:36.320 --> 02:04:40.160
before Carl was killed and the
police are fully aware of that,

02:04:40.240 --> 02:04:42.280
they found the gun.

02:04:42.320 --> 02:04:46.880
- Producer: Bert once stored
some antiques, copper and brass,

02:04:46.920 --> 02:04:50.520
in a friend's shed and was
spotted in the shed removing

02:04:50.600 --> 02:04:51.720
them.

02:04:51.760 --> 02:04:53.800
He was asked to move
them which he did,

02:04:53.880 --> 02:04:56.960
this happens after Carl's death,
but before the death of Hubert

02:04:57.040 --> 02:04:58.560
Wilkes.

02:04:58.600 --> 02:05:01.440
When Janet later asked
Bert about this in prison,

02:05:01.520 --> 02:05:04.600
he told her that they
were buried near the A449

02:05:04.640 --> 02:05:07.880
[INDISCERNIBLE] Road, and she
was never to tell anybody about

02:05:07.960 --> 02:05:09.040
that, ever.

02:05:09.080 --> 02:05:12.440
- Bert Spencer: I do not
agree with that comment,

02:05:12.480 --> 02:05:14.480
it is nonsense.

02:05:14.560 --> 02:05:17.880
- Producer: Janet finally
says, after many years,

02:05:17.920 --> 02:05:21.400
she has reached the conclusion
that Bert probably did kill Carl

02:05:21.480 --> 02:05:25.000
Bridgewater, though at the time
she didn't want to believe it of

02:05:25.080 --> 02:05:26.000
her husband.

02:05:26.080 --> 02:05:29.440
- Bert Spencer: I am
aware of her opinion,

02:05:29.520 --> 02:05:31.360
like many others in the
world, she is welcome

02:05:31.400 --> 02:05:33.000
to her opinion.

02:05:33.080 --> 02:05:36.600
It happens I don't
agree with it.

02:05:36.640 --> 02:05:39.640
(music playing)

02:05:39.720 --> 02:05:45.280
I promise you, if you bring
evidence that can be accepted in

02:05:45.320 --> 02:05:51.480
a high court, that what you say
is true, I promise you, Janet,

02:05:51.520 --> 02:05:53.480
no harm will come to you.

02:05:53.520 --> 02:05:58.000
No threats will be made to
you, but I would--I am ready to

02:05:58.080 --> 02:06:06.280
instigate my own kamikaze method
and I promise you; you would be,

02:06:06.360 --> 02:06:11.080
I would make sure you
are immediately arrested,

02:06:11.160 --> 02:06:17.160
charged and found guilty of
conspiring in the courts of

02:06:17.240 --> 02:06:22.280
justice for all these years, of
putting four men behind bars,

02:06:22.320 --> 02:06:28.320
unnecessarily, and
more seriously, Janet,

02:06:28.360 --> 02:06:33.360
being an accessory
after the fact.

02:06:33.400 --> 02:06:36.600
Regardless, of all the
allegations that keep coming my

02:06:36.680 --> 02:06:45.720
way, hear this, I will never,
ever be a scapegoat for the

02:06:45.800 --> 02:06:48.640
murder of Carl
Bridgewater, by golly,

02:06:48.680 --> 02:06:51.920
I've had enough
of this snipping.

02:06:52.000 --> 02:06:55.760
I've had enough, Janet,
and when I've had enough,

02:06:55.840 --> 02:06:59.320
I may well institute kamikaze.

02:06:59.360 --> 02:07:04.080
(music playing)

